WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:04.738
(peaceful acoustic guitar music)

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:13.480
- [Interviewer] How would you
describe the Gulf of Mexico?

00:00:15.115 --> 00:00:16.349
- Paradise.

00:00:16.349 --> 00:00:18.051
- Subtropical paradise.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.185
- Paradise.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.587
- Grand Isle was paradise.

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:21.921
- It's a sportsman's paradise.

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.590
- Diverse, abundant.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:24.557
- Strong.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:25.425
- Resilient.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:26.526
- Bountiful.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:27.460
- Beautiful.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.628
- Magnificent.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:30.029
- Perfection.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:31.531
- Home.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.101
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.838
- I think that we're facing a situation

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.174
where there is relatively little oil,

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:44.677
where there is not enough to
warrant the substantial risk.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:49.215
- Nobody knows how many
millions of barrels,

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:52.752
billions of barrels of oil, and how many--

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.488
- Our industries have
pioneered the technology used

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:59.759
in environmentally responsible drilling.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:02.595
- And they were not
prepared to take the risk.

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:04.030
We are not prepared to take the risk

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.001
that the Secretary of
Interior is very focused on.

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:10.303
- And we'll drill for the
billions of barrels of oil

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:13.806
that we have right now
warehoused underground

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:16.009
including our resources offshore.

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:18.511
We will drill here--
- Drill, baby, drill,

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:22.282
and drill now.
(audience cheering)

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:28.521
- So today we're announcing the expansion

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:31.824
of offshore oil and gas exploration

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:34.627
but in ways that balance
the need to harness--

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:37.864
(audience cheering)

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:41.200
- Drill, baby, drill? (chuckles)

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.903
Drill, baby, drill!
(crowds cheering)

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:44.971
Drill, baby, drill!

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:48.274
(explosion thunders)

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:51.077
- [Reporter] 11 workers are
still missing and feared dead

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:54.080
of the rig that exploded
on Tuesday has now sunk,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.183
threatening a major oil spill
off the Louisiana coast.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:04.757
- [Man] We are at the
beginning of the end,

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.860
but there are still a
lot of uncertainties.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:13.666
- [Narrator] On the
morning of April 20, 2010,

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:15.968
there was a disaster

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.738
aboard the Deepwater Horizon drilling rig,

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:22.942
which killed 11 people and
leaked massive amounts of oil

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.011
into the Gulf of Mexico.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:27.814
The Gulf,

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:31.450
which is the lifeblood for so
many hardworking Americans,

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.154
had become severely damaged and polluted.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.425
What caused this tragedy?

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.996
Who was ultimately responsible?

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:47.133
And how long was it going to take

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:50.002
for this once bountiful ecosystem

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:52.705
to bounce back from
this severe devastation?

00:02:55.274 --> 00:02:58.377
To find the answers to
these difficult questions,

00:02:58.377 --> 00:03:01.147
we will need to begin this investigation

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:04.283
by taking a closer look into BP's history.

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:08.321
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:11.190
- I believe that the fundamentals of BP,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.192
our strategy, our assets,

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:15.027
and the highest standards of our people,

00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:17.597
are something we should all be proud of.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:20.866
- [Narrator] In 1995, BP hired mergers

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:25.104
and acquisitions guru John
Browne as it's new CEO.

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:28.307
Browne had one mission:

00:03:28.307 --> 00:03:32.244
to make BP the largest
oil conglomerate possible

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:33.813
at any cost.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.215
Two years later,

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:40.619
he initiated what was then
the largest merger in history,

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:45.257
the $61 billion takeover of Amoco,

00:03:45.257 --> 00:03:50.262
Arco, Burma Castrol, and
several other companies,

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:54.300
making BP the third
largest private oil company

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.401
in the world.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.270
It was these mergers

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:02.007
that caused BP to face the enormous task

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:04.777
of upgrading numerous aging facilities,

00:04:05.978 --> 00:04:07.947
managing substantial debt,

00:04:09.048 --> 00:04:12.418
along with integrating
thousands of new employees.

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:16.322
You might then be asking yourself

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:19.225
how this affected BP's safety culture.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:21.427
Not well.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:27.266
In fact, the EPA cited BP
as the largest polluter

00:04:27.266 --> 00:04:29.368
in the United States.

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.638
This was even more evident when
studying their long history

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:33.706
of disasters.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.375
The list is shocking.

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:40.045
(intrepid thoughtful music)

00:06:13.605 --> 00:06:15.908
- Since you took over as CEO of BP,

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:19.444
that safe, reliable operations
are a number one priority.

00:06:19.444 --> 00:06:20.379
Correct?

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:21.213
- That is correct.

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:23.815
- And you've been CEO
for the past three years.

00:06:23.815 --> 00:06:25.317
Correct?
- Correct.

00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:27.953
- Then explain to us why

00:06:27.953 --> 00:06:32.591
between June of 2007 and February of 2010,

00:06:32.591 --> 00:06:35.627
the Occupational Health
and Safety Administration

00:06:35.627 --> 00:06:39.932
checked 55 oil refineries
operating in the US.

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:43.669
Two of those 55 are owned by BP,

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:47.639
and BP's refineries
racked up 760 citations

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:51.176
for egregiously willful safety violations,

00:06:51.176 --> 00:06:56.148
accounting for 97% of the worst
and most serious violations

00:06:56.148 --> 00:06:58.350
that OSHA monitors in the workplace.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.852
That doesn't sound like
a culture of safety.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:03.755
- [Narrator] In summary,

00:07:03.755 --> 00:07:08.293
in the United States
alone from 1990 to 2010,

00:07:08.293 --> 00:07:12.364
BP was responsible for 8000 spills,

00:07:13.598 --> 00:07:16.802
217 injuries,

00:07:16.802 --> 00:07:20.605
and the deaths of 38 of its own employees.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:25.210
BP has also been charged
with numerous felonies

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:28.347
and has become infamous
for cutting corners

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:31.116
in order to increase shareholder profits

00:07:31.116 --> 00:07:33.218
by any means necessary.

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:38.890
After learning of all BP's
willful safety violations,

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:43.595
deaths, injuries, environmental disasters,

00:07:43.595 --> 00:07:45.697
and felony charges,

00:07:45.697 --> 00:07:49.201
it was astonishing to
learn of the billions

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:52.270
in US government contracts
they were still receiving.

00:07:53.638 --> 00:07:57.409
Why was the United States
government continuing to fund

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:59.945
this blatantly egregious behavior?

00:08:01.313 --> 00:08:05.317
To answer this, we must
look further into the past

00:08:05.317 --> 00:08:07.452
when the need for domestic oil productions

00:08:07.452 --> 00:08:09.421
became much more prevalent

00:08:09.421 --> 00:08:12.023
within the United States government.

00:08:12.023 --> 00:08:15.660
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:16.495
- Good evening.

00:08:16.495 --> 00:08:18.563
The Middle East war produced developments

00:08:18.563 --> 00:08:20.332
all over the world today.

00:08:20.332 --> 00:08:23.435
The oil producing countries
of the Arab world decided

00:08:23.435 --> 00:08:26.004
to use their oil as a political weapon.

00:08:26.004 --> 00:08:29.441
They will reduce oil
production by 5% a month

00:08:29.441 --> 00:08:32.978
until the Israelis withdraw
from occupied territories.

00:08:32.978 --> 00:08:34.846
If the Arab countries keep that pledge,

00:08:34.846 --> 00:08:39.784
it would reduce their production
by almost 50% in one year.

00:08:39.784 --> 00:08:42.287
- [Narrator] As a result
of US support of Israel

00:08:42.287 --> 00:08:44.356
in the Yom Kippur war,

00:08:44.356 --> 00:08:49.327
in 1973 OPEC announced that
it would cut production

00:08:49.794 --> 00:08:52.631
and place an embargo
on the export of crude

00:08:52.631 --> 00:08:53.999
to Western countries.

00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:58.169
This caused a global spike in oil prices

00:08:58.169 --> 00:09:00.505
unlike anything the world had ever seen.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.274
After the embargo,

00:09:03.274 --> 00:09:07.445
consumers were forced to wait
in long lines at the pump.

00:09:07.445 --> 00:09:11.916
Station owners set limits on
the amount customers could buy

00:09:11.916 --> 00:09:14.719
and frequently ran out of gas.

00:09:14.719 --> 00:09:18.857
Regardless of the fact that
America imported less than 50%

00:09:18.857 --> 00:09:21.292
of its oil from Arab nations.

00:09:22.527 --> 00:09:26.498
During this time, oil
prices increased 330%.

00:09:28.533 --> 00:09:31.970
Big oil used the embargo
as political motivation

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:34.572
to open new lands for domestic drilling;

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.742
however, this increase
in domestic production

00:09:37.742 --> 00:09:41.346
would not bring any real
relief at the pumps,

00:09:41.346 --> 00:09:44.749
as the oil companies decided
to keep crude prices high

00:09:44.749 --> 00:09:46.718
to maximize profits.

00:09:47.619 --> 00:09:51.556
This had a devastating impact
on economies across the globe.

00:09:52.791 --> 00:09:56.628
The US responded by
creating a new energy policy

00:09:56.628 --> 00:09:59.697
and began to look into
alternative energy solutions.

00:10:00.832 --> 00:10:03.601
But sadly, by the end of the 1970s,

00:10:03.601 --> 00:10:07.005
the lessons of the oil embargo
were all but forgotten,

00:10:07.005 --> 00:10:08.640
and Americans had reverted back

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.742
to their previous consumption habits.

00:10:12.343 --> 00:10:14.779
Ultimately, the embargo changed the way

00:10:14.779 --> 00:10:18.383
the United States viewed its
dependence on foreign oil,

00:10:18.383 --> 00:10:20.752
forcing the nation to take a serious look

00:10:20.752 --> 00:10:23.388
into drilling in its own backyard.

00:10:24.222 --> 00:10:26.457
- [Newscaster] Reagan
is our projected winner.

00:10:26.457 --> 00:10:29.928
Ronald Wilson Reagan of California,

00:10:29.928 --> 00:10:32.597
a sports announcer, a film actor,

00:10:32.597 --> 00:10:37.268
a governor of California
is our projected winner.

00:10:37.268 --> 00:10:40.405
- [Narrator] Ronald Reagan
was elected president in 1981.

00:10:41.539 --> 00:10:45.009
He made the initial push
to open domestic drilling,

00:10:45.009 --> 00:10:47.278
allowing free markets to reign

00:10:47.278 --> 00:10:50.715
and trusting that big
industry would act morally.

00:10:50.715 --> 00:10:53.518
Under Reagan, the Federal Trade Commission

00:10:53.518 --> 00:10:58.189
took on a new dismissive approach
toward corporate mergers.

00:10:58.189 --> 00:11:00.958
This environment fostered
the biggest takeovers

00:11:00.958 --> 00:11:05.229
in corporate history: Exxon and Mobil,

00:11:05.229 --> 00:11:07.231
Chevron and Texaco,

00:11:07.231 --> 00:11:09.767
Conoco and Phillips,

00:11:09.767 --> 00:11:13.237
and BP with Amoco and then Arco,

00:11:14.238 --> 00:11:16.140
creating the largest corporations

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:17.842
in the history of the world.

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:21.746
The mergers allowed Big Oil to resurface

00:11:21.746 --> 00:11:24.782
as a major owner of oil

00:11:24.782 --> 00:11:26.784
and allowed them to exercise their control

00:11:26.784 --> 00:11:29.687
over prices through the futures market,

00:11:29.687 --> 00:11:32.256
replacing OPEC as the
principal determinate

00:11:32.256 --> 00:11:34.425
of the price of crude.

00:11:34.425 --> 00:11:35.960
- Ronald Reagan made promises

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:37.929
that we're gonna deregulate everything,

00:11:37.929 --> 00:11:40.631
we're gonna get government
out of your life entirely,

00:11:40.631 --> 00:11:43.434
and that private industry
is gonna do a better job,

00:11:43.434 --> 00:11:46.237
and we're all gonna be
happy with private industry.

00:11:46.237 --> 00:11:48.406
Private industry's gonna be
able to deliver the mail better,

00:11:48.406 --> 00:11:49.874
they're gonna be able
to give you healthcare,

00:11:49.874 --> 00:11:51.542
they're gonna be able to
take care of your children,

00:11:51.542 --> 00:11:53.511
they're gonna be able
to take your prisons,

00:11:53.511 --> 00:11:55.480
they're gonna take care
of everything for you.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:58.649
It was the biggest lie
that has ever been told

00:11:58.649 --> 00:12:00.518
to the American public.

00:12:00.518 --> 00:12:03.988
- I am suggesting that there
are literally thousands

00:12:03.988 --> 00:12:06.858
of unnecessary regulations

00:12:06.858 --> 00:12:09.393
that invade every facet of business

00:12:09.393 --> 00:12:12.330
and, indeed, very much
of our personal lives

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.799
that are unnecessary, that
government can do without,

00:12:14.799 --> 00:12:17.201
that have added $130 billion to the cost

00:12:17.201 --> 00:12:18.936
of production in this country

00:12:18.936 --> 00:12:23.774
and that are contributing
their part to inflation,

00:12:23.774 --> 00:12:26.777
and I would like to see
us a little more free,

00:12:26.777 --> 00:12:27.979
as we once were.

00:12:27.979 --> 00:12:32.049
We have only leased out
and begun to explore 2%

00:12:32.049 --> 00:12:35.252
of out outer continental shelf for oil.

00:12:35.252 --> 00:12:38.623
Probably 70% of the potential oil

00:12:38.623 --> 00:12:43.260
in the United States in
probably hidden in those lands,

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:45.196
and no one is allowed
to even go and explore

00:12:45.196 --> 00:12:47.064
to find out if it is there.

00:12:47.064 --> 00:12:49.867
- [Narrator] Big Oil also began
taking drilling operations

00:12:49.867 --> 00:12:51.635
into deeper waters

00:12:51.635 --> 00:12:54.138
where their exploits
became much more risky.

00:12:55.339 --> 00:12:57.875
The government downplayed this risk,

00:12:57.875 --> 00:13:01.012
and the American public had
been convinced they could rely

00:13:01.012 --> 00:13:03.547
on the oil industry to do what was best

00:13:03.547 --> 00:13:06.517
for the environment and our way of life.

00:13:08.018 --> 00:13:11.389
Reagan would pass the
torch to his number two,

00:13:11.389 --> 00:13:15.459
and finally the oil industry
had one of their own in office.

00:13:18.229 --> 00:13:19.397
Two months later:

00:13:21.098 --> 00:13:23.300
- An oil tanker smashed into a reef

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:26.737
near Valdez, Alaska, today
causing the worst oil spill

00:13:26.737 --> 00:13:29.373
since the Alaska pipeline
opened 12 years ago.

00:13:30.407 --> 00:13:34.078
- [Narrator] It is interesting
to note that BP owned Alyeska

00:13:34.078 --> 00:13:37.047
the company in charge of
the Exxon Valdez cleanup.

00:13:38.549 --> 00:13:41.919
By studying the fallout
from the Exxon Valdez spill

00:13:41.919 --> 00:13:44.321
and the following cleanup process,

00:13:44.321 --> 00:13:47.258
we can get an accurate
idea of what lies ahead

00:13:47.258 --> 00:13:50.060
for the Gulf ecosystem and its residents.

00:13:51.662 --> 00:13:54.899
- We felt that, by mentioning Alyeska,

00:13:54.899 --> 00:13:57.067
people would look into
it and discover, oh, yes,

00:13:57.067 --> 00:13:59.203
it was dominated by BP.

00:13:59.203 --> 00:14:02.239
So we didn't name those names.

00:14:02.239 --> 00:14:04.408
In retrospect, I wish dearly we had

00:14:05.242 --> 00:14:07.177
because it could've caused a change

00:14:07.177 --> 00:14:09.547
in the internal corporate climate.

00:14:09.547 --> 00:14:14.151
Transparency, public attention,
makes a huge difference,

00:14:14.151 --> 00:14:15.719
and in the Exxon Valdez case,

00:14:15.719 --> 00:14:18.255
the attention was diverted almost entirely

00:14:18.255 --> 00:14:20.891
on a captain who had had a few drinks.

00:14:20.891 --> 00:14:22.660
- Another think we believed was,

00:14:23.961 --> 00:14:27.998
there'll only be short-term
harm in the ecosystem.

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:32.236
We actually questioned
the scientists on that,

00:14:32.236 --> 00:14:36.573
and it turned out that that
was based on a 1970s science

00:14:36.573 --> 00:14:40.377
that had been done for the Clean
Air Act and Clean Water Act

00:14:40.377 --> 00:14:43.714
and the scientists didn't have
any reason not to believe it.

00:14:44.581 --> 00:14:46.350
It's sorta like the world is flat

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:49.219
until you suddenly discover
the world is round,

00:14:49.219 --> 00:14:51.655
and then everything changes, right?

00:14:51.655 --> 00:14:54.858
So everything changed with
the Exxon Valdez oil spill.

00:14:54.858 --> 00:14:59.163
The oil that washed ashore
happened to be in the nurseries

00:14:59.163 --> 00:15:01.298
for the pink salmon and the herring,

00:15:01.298 --> 00:15:04.034
and the fishermen and
the native people said,

00:15:04.034 --> 00:15:05.335
"How can this be?

00:15:05.335 --> 00:15:08.539
"Here are these little eggs
and here's all this oil.

00:15:08.539 --> 00:15:09.506
"What gonna happen?

00:15:09.506 --> 00:15:11.708
"So we're gonna wait, we're gonna wait."

00:15:11.708 --> 00:15:13.210
We picked four years

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:16.080
because that was when the
herring should have matured

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.815
and become adults was in four years,

00:15:17.815 --> 00:15:21.852
and it's when the pink salmon,
the ones that survived,

00:15:21.852 --> 00:15:25.489
their offspring should
have come back as adults.

00:15:25.489 --> 00:15:26.890
One of our questions was,

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:28.859
"What's gonna happen with
those little baby fish?

00:15:28.859 --> 00:15:31.195
"Are they gonna be able
to reproduce normally?"

00:15:31.195 --> 00:15:32.329
So we picked four years.

00:15:32.329 --> 00:15:33.564
And it turns out, in four years,

00:15:33.564 --> 00:15:36.266
that's when the ecosystem
collapsed in Prince William Sound.

00:15:36.266 --> 00:15:37.968
And all the scientists were saying,

00:15:37.968 --> 00:15:39.570
"How can this be? How can this be?"

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:42.539
So the world was all of a sudden round,

00:15:42.539 --> 00:15:45.275
and it took the scientists
another eight years

00:15:45.275 --> 00:15:46.977
to do all the laboratory studies

00:15:46.977 --> 00:15:49.546
and the field studies to sort of validate

00:15:49.546 --> 00:15:54.051
the new world order that oil is more toxic

00:15:54.051 --> 00:15:57.054
than the scientists thought in the 1970s.

00:15:57.054 --> 00:15:59.990
- Basically, herring in that area crashed

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:03.360
and never came back after the spill.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:08.365
We lost about, estimated
250,000 birds, seabirds,

00:16:11.435 --> 00:16:13.670
and we also lost eagles,
quite a few bald eagles

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:15.906
'cause they feed on dead carrion.

00:16:15.906 --> 00:16:19.343
So anything oiled was munch time for them.

00:16:19.343 --> 00:16:23.113
And we lost a pod of orcas,

00:16:23.113 --> 00:16:26.249
and we lost a good many
sea otters as well.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:35.792
- The one group had brain dysfunction,

00:16:35.792 --> 00:16:37.728
and they had short-term memory loss,

00:16:37.728 --> 00:16:39.496
and they couldn't concentrate,

00:16:39.496 --> 00:16:42.599
and they felt like they
were in a fog all the time.

00:16:45.035 --> 00:16:48.071
Then there was another
group that had asthma,

00:16:48.071 --> 00:16:52.109
and then another group
with heart irregularities,

00:16:52.109 --> 00:16:55.612
and then just general
malaise, chronic fatigue,

00:16:58.348 --> 00:17:00.350
just couldn't function very well at all.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:03.587
A lot of muscle aches, joint
aches, and things like that.

00:17:03.587 --> 00:17:06.456
- [Narrator] Exxon spilled
nearly 11 million gallons

00:17:06.456 --> 00:17:08.391
of crude into Prince William Sound.

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:13.730
Over 500,000 birds and
wildlife were killed,

00:17:13.730 --> 00:17:16.533
and more than 6000
people would be diagnosed

00:17:16.533 --> 00:17:19.402
with chronic illnesses
believed to be caused

00:17:19.402 --> 00:17:20.871
by the cleanup process.

00:17:22.906 --> 00:17:24.908
It was becoming clear that the effects

00:17:24.908 --> 00:17:28.879
of this disaster were going
to be serious and longterm.

00:17:30.614 --> 00:17:31.615
What is in store

00:17:31.615 --> 00:17:34.251
for the Gulf Coast residents
working on the cleanup?

00:17:35.418 --> 00:17:37.787
Are they going to experience
the same health issues

00:17:37.787 --> 00:17:39.556
in the next few decades

00:17:39.556 --> 00:17:42.058
that the Valdez workers
are dealing with now?

00:17:44.594 --> 00:17:48.198
This era of deregulation led
to the expansion of power

00:17:48.198 --> 00:17:50.467
for Big Oil in the United States.

00:17:51.334 --> 00:17:53.103
The interaction between corporate

00:17:53.103 --> 00:17:56.373
and governmental agendas
had become blurred

00:17:56.373 --> 00:17:59.309
and enabled corporations
to influence elections

00:17:59.309 --> 00:18:02.245
and US policy making like never before.

00:18:03.914 --> 00:18:05.982
Unfortunately, with the expansion

00:18:05.982 --> 00:18:08.318
of lobbyism in the late '90s,

00:18:08.318 --> 00:18:11.588
this type of corporate power
and influence would continue

00:18:11.588 --> 00:18:15.325
to grow exponentially throughout
administrations to come.

00:18:16.526 --> 00:18:20.497
- Basically, this is an
industry that has been dominant

00:18:20.497 --> 00:18:22.432
in US politics for a very long time,

00:18:22.432 --> 00:18:25.769
but that dominance actually
is only recently been

00:18:25.769 --> 00:18:28.672
as strong as it is now in modern history,

00:18:28.672 --> 00:18:31.741
and that really began
around the year 2000,

00:18:31.741 --> 00:18:35.378
and it really began with
the coming into office

00:18:35.378 --> 00:18:38.114
of the Bush administration
in the level of extremes

00:18:38.114 --> 00:18:39.649
that we're witnessing right now.

00:18:39.649 --> 00:18:42.185
And those extremes are:

00:18:42.185 --> 00:18:45.588
the Bush administration
essentially opening up new avenues

00:18:45.588 --> 00:18:49.392
of operation for BP or
for the oil industry,

00:18:49.392 --> 00:18:51.728
dramatically reducing regulation,

00:18:51.728 --> 00:18:53.863
dramatically reducing oversight,

00:18:53.863 --> 00:18:56.533
dramatically reducing enforcement.

00:18:56.533 --> 00:18:58.401
- [Narrator] The oil
industry spent more money

00:18:58.401 --> 00:19:01.571
on the George W. Bush
administration in 2000

00:19:01.571 --> 00:19:04.073
than any election in history.

00:19:05.875 --> 00:19:09.846
For their generosity,
they received a president,

00:19:12.115 --> 00:19:13.249
Vice President,

00:19:14.884 --> 00:19:16.486
Secretary of Interior,

00:19:18.855 --> 00:19:20.423
Secretary of Commerce,

00:19:21.658 --> 00:19:23.059
and Secretary of State

00:19:23.927 --> 00:19:26.863
who were all former oil company officials.

00:19:29.532 --> 00:19:32.535
This administration
did more than any other

00:19:32.535 --> 00:19:34.671
to further the interests of Big Oil

00:19:34.671 --> 00:19:38.608
through deregulation, lack of enforcement,

00:19:38.608 --> 00:19:41.978
new access to public lands and oceans,

00:19:41.978 --> 00:19:45.748
subsidies, tax breaks, and war.

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.819
In fact, the revolving
door between government

00:19:49.819 --> 00:19:52.922
and the oil industry is all too familiar.

00:19:55.458 --> 00:19:57.660
- What we do know is that, basically,

00:19:57.660 --> 00:19:59.329
within the first few
days of taking office,

00:19:59.329 --> 00:20:02.665
the Bush administration
gave Cheney the right

00:20:02.665 --> 00:20:03.833
to make this taskforce.

00:20:03.833 --> 00:20:05.768
He was allowed to call
all his best buddies

00:20:05.768 --> 00:20:07.904
from his days in the oil industry over,

00:20:07.904 --> 00:20:09.973
and not just oil but coal and gas

00:20:09.973 --> 00:20:11.140
and all these other fossil fuels.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:12.408
But they called them over,

00:20:12.408 --> 00:20:14.310
and they had all these
series of meetings here

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:17.046
over months to come up with a policy plan

00:20:17.046 --> 00:20:19.048
that would go on to guide
then next eight years

00:20:19.048 --> 00:20:20.416
of this administration.

00:20:21.818 --> 00:20:24.253
This taskforce document
they put out was basically

00:20:24.253 --> 00:20:26.689
a wishlist for the oil,
gas, and coal industries.

00:20:26.689 --> 00:20:29.926
It was asking for ways
to weaken regulations.

00:20:29.926 --> 00:20:31.828
It was asking for numerous incentives to,

00:20:31.828 --> 00:20:34.030
among other things, drill offshore.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:37.400
- You name it, if it was
supervised, if it was overseen,

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:39.736
if it was regulated by
the Federal Government,

00:20:39.736 --> 00:20:43.272
Cheney, with his marvelous
bureaucratic talent,

00:20:43.272 --> 00:20:46.709
moved in and, essentially,
replaced the people

00:20:46.709 --> 00:20:48.311
who were in the positions

00:20:48.311 --> 00:20:52.014
that were central to this
regulation, this oversight,

00:20:52.014 --> 00:20:54.917
with people who were either lobbyists

00:20:54.917 --> 00:20:56.819
for the industry being regulated

00:20:56.819 --> 00:20:59.288
or executives from that industry.

00:21:00.323 --> 00:21:02.758
- [Narrator] The Bush Cheney
administration enacted one

00:21:02.758 --> 00:21:07.063
of the most pro-oil, anti-environment
pieces of legislation

00:21:07.063 --> 00:21:11.734
in history, the Energy Policy Act of 2005.

00:21:12.702 --> 00:21:16.005
In particular, Section 390 of the Act

00:21:16.005 --> 00:21:18.174
dramatically extended the circumstances

00:21:18.174 --> 00:21:21.310
under which new drilling
permits could be approved

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:23.846
without further environmental review.

00:21:25.214 --> 00:21:28.784
The Minerals Management Service, or MMS,

00:21:28.784 --> 00:21:31.320
which is responsible for
the inspection and oversight

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:34.957
of energy companies approved
the project using this type

00:21:34.957 --> 00:21:36.592
of categorical exclusion.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:41.130
In fact, BP was lobbying to expand the use

00:21:41.130 --> 00:21:43.699
of such exemptions less than two weeks

00:21:43.699 --> 00:21:46.102
before the Deepwater Horizon disaster.

00:21:47.303 --> 00:21:49.905
- [Col. Wilkerson] He destroyed
the regulatory mechanisms

00:21:49.905 --> 00:21:51.274
in America.
- Give us some examples.

00:21:51.274 --> 00:21:56.279
- Cheney left, I'm told,
somewhere around 1600 plus people

00:21:56.479 --> 00:21:59.415
in the administration
whom he had converted

00:21:59.415 --> 00:22:03.352
from being political or he had
recruited as civil service,

00:22:03.352 --> 00:22:06.088
he converted them to civil
service if they were political,

00:22:06.088 --> 00:22:08.157
and left them in these positions

00:22:08.157 --> 00:22:11.227
that are very key to
regulation and oversight.

00:22:11.227 --> 00:22:14.697
And those people will take
a year, a year and a half,

00:22:14.697 --> 00:22:17.033
maybe even two years for
the Obama administration

00:22:17.033 --> 00:22:18.534
to root out and get rid of.

00:22:19.435 --> 00:22:20.503
- [Narrator] It was astonishing

00:22:20.503 --> 00:22:23.672
that a federal regulatory
agency was corrupted

00:22:23.672 --> 00:22:26.375
so intrinsically by the industry

00:22:26.375 --> 00:22:28.110
they were suppose to be regulating.

00:22:29.512 --> 00:22:30.980
How could this have happened?

00:22:32.548 --> 00:22:35.851
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:22:35.851 --> 00:22:39.221
In 1982, Congress passed the Federal Oil

00:22:39.221 --> 00:22:41.757
and Gas Royalty Management Act,

00:22:41.757 --> 00:22:44.326
which mandates the
protection of the environment

00:22:44.326 --> 00:22:46.495
and conservation of federal lands

00:22:46.495 --> 00:22:49.832
in the course of developing
oil and gas facilities.

00:22:51.567 --> 00:22:55.805
Reagan chose James Watt to be
his Secretary of the Interior.

00:22:56.705 --> 00:22:59.375
Watt was a controversial figure,

00:22:59.375 --> 00:23:02.211
well known for his
anti-environmental policies.

00:23:03.779 --> 00:23:06.982
- It was the Federal Oil and
Gas Royalty Management Act

00:23:06.982 --> 00:23:08.250
that set it up,

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:12.321
but it was specifically
to not only exploit

00:23:12.321 --> 00:23:14.256
the offshore resources,

00:23:14.256 --> 00:23:15.624
but to protect the environment.

00:23:15.624 --> 00:23:18.661
And they certainly have done
a good job on the one hand

00:23:18.661 --> 00:23:20.062
with exploiting the resources,

00:23:20.062 --> 00:23:21.997
but they have done a very poor job

00:23:21.997 --> 00:23:23.532
with protecting the environment.

00:23:23.532 --> 00:23:27.536
And you have to remember that
all of these resources are

00:23:27.536 --> 00:23:30.205
in the common trust,
they're the public trust.

00:23:30.205 --> 00:23:31.040
- In spite of the fact

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:33.509
that you read it
repeatedly, over and over,

00:23:33.509 --> 00:23:36.412
the facts are not one
lease or well was drilled

00:23:36.412 --> 00:23:39.815
in a park or wilderness area
during my predecessor's time

00:23:39.815 --> 00:23:41.817
or my time or in the successor's time

00:23:41.817 --> 00:23:43.953
'cause it's just not desirable or legal.

00:23:43.953 --> 00:23:45.287
- Did anybody go inside one of these?

00:23:45.287 --> 00:23:46.822
You know, they were
doing that, weren't they?

00:23:46.822 --> 00:23:48.991
Start from the outside
and go in and underneath.

00:23:48.991 --> 00:23:50.526
Boy, you were sneaky, gotta watch these--

00:23:50.526 --> 00:23:54.029
- In New Mexico we did one
well, and we slant drilled.

00:23:54.029 --> 00:23:55.597
They were outside the boundaries there,

00:23:55.597 --> 00:23:56.899
and they came drilling in,

00:23:56.899 --> 00:23:59.268
snaking that old noodle
right down in there

00:23:59.268 --> 00:24:01.670
and hopefully found oil

00:24:01.670 --> 00:24:03.272
so we don't have to be so dependent

00:24:03.272 --> 00:24:05.207
upon those Persian Gulf states.

00:24:05.207 --> 00:24:09.311
I don't want to have America
in a hostage situation

00:24:09.311 --> 00:24:11.947
to the Arabs surrounding the Persian Gulf.

00:24:11.947 --> 00:24:15.117
- Reagan invented James Watt, OK?

00:24:15.117 --> 00:24:16.452
He invented James Watt

00:24:16.452 --> 00:24:19.054
because he knew what James Watt was.

00:24:19.054 --> 00:24:23.292
He was nothing more than
an ideologue buffoon.

00:24:23.292 --> 00:24:26.428
Not only was he not
qualified to do the job,

00:24:26.428 --> 00:24:29.264
his ideology didn't
allow him to do the job.

00:24:29.264 --> 00:24:33.102
He came from an industry
that hated government.

00:24:33.102 --> 00:24:36.438
The mining industry where
he come from around Wyoming,

00:24:36.438 --> 00:24:37.940
they hated government

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:40.976
because government was making
them actually pay taxes

00:24:40.976 --> 00:24:43.312
for taking our natural resources.

00:24:43.312 --> 00:24:45.314
Government was making
them, when they drilled,

00:24:45.314 --> 00:24:48.617
do it without destroying
our waterways and our air.

00:24:48.617 --> 00:24:51.453
- [Narrator] Watt cut funds
for environment programs

00:24:51.453 --> 00:24:53.722
and ensured there would be less regulation

00:24:53.722 --> 00:24:55.657
on the federal level.

00:24:55.657 --> 00:24:58.293
He had no concern for conservation

00:24:58.293 --> 00:25:01.029
and proposed that, by
the turn of the century,

00:25:01.029 --> 00:25:05.500
all 80 million acres of US
wilderness become available

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:07.269
for drilling and mining.

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:09.771
- Matter of fact, there's something

00:25:09.771 --> 00:25:10.706
that you can go back and look.

00:25:10.706 --> 00:25:12.975
It's called the Sagebrush Rebellion.

00:25:12.975 --> 00:25:16.645
The Sagebrush Rebellion was
exactly those mining people

00:25:16.645 --> 00:25:19.648
and that Western industries,
the Coors family.

00:25:19.648 --> 00:25:21.149
It was Richard Mellon Scaife.

00:25:21.149 --> 00:25:24.353
It was the Koch brothers.

00:25:24.353 --> 00:25:29.358
All of those guys said, "We are
going to change government."

00:25:29.358 --> 00:25:33.495
Ronald Reagan was the guy
they put up to do that.

00:25:33.495 --> 00:25:36.031
Ronald Reagan was a man
who'd had no history

00:25:36.031 --> 00:25:37.833
of accomplishing anything, really.

00:25:37.833 --> 00:25:41.069
They put him in office as
governor of California.

00:25:42.437 --> 00:25:44.773
This is a guy who sold Borax soap

00:25:46.274 --> 00:25:48.210
for General Electric's television.

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:49.044
I mean, what was it?

00:25:49.044 --> 00:25:49.978
I forget the name of the show,

00:25:49.978 --> 00:25:52.547
but he had some Western cowboy show,

00:25:52.547 --> 00:25:54.082
and he sold Borax soap.

00:25:54.082 --> 00:25:56.718
So they take this guy,
he has a great face,

00:25:57.619 --> 00:26:00.789
he talks well, and we're
gonna put him in government.

00:26:00.789 --> 00:26:02.724
And so they put him in government,

00:26:02.724 --> 00:26:05.260
but he's their boy, you understand.

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:08.597
Ronald Reagan is corporate America's boy.

00:26:08.597 --> 00:26:10.465
And so as corporate America's boy,

00:26:10.465 --> 00:26:12.401
they ask him to deliver something:

00:26:12.401 --> 00:26:14.035
Take government off our back.

00:26:14.035 --> 00:26:14.903
How do they do that?

00:26:14.903 --> 00:26:16.738
They create new agencies.

00:26:16.738 --> 00:26:18.874
One of them was Mineral Management.

00:26:18.874 --> 00:26:21.910
Mineral Management was created by Reagan

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:26.915
for ideologue dimwits like James Watt.

00:26:27.816 --> 00:26:31.820
So James Watt, then, does
exactly what we'd expect.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:33.688
He hires people just like him.

00:26:33.688 --> 00:26:36.958
From the top to bottom, they are dimwits.

00:26:36.958 --> 00:26:39.928
They're intellectual lightweights,
and more importantly,

00:26:39.928 --> 00:26:42.531
they are people who are looking for jobs

00:26:42.531 --> 00:26:44.766
because they can't get jobs anywhere else.

00:26:44.766 --> 00:26:47.469
I mean, they're minimally qualified,

00:26:47.469 --> 00:26:51.473
and every time they're
supposed to be regulating,

00:26:51.473 --> 00:26:52.707
what really they're doing is,

00:26:52.707 --> 00:26:54.843
they're saying at the dinner table

00:26:54.843 --> 00:26:56.645
with this corporation they're
supposed to be regulating,

00:26:56.645 --> 00:26:58.847
"Are you gonna give me a job?"

00:26:58.847 --> 00:27:01.483
And so the quid pro quo, the trade off is,

00:27:01.483 --> 00:27:02.817
if you're gonna give me a job,

00:27:02.817 --> 00:27:05.687
I'm gonna let you do what
the hell you wanna do.

00:27:05.687 --> 00:27:07.489
- [Narrator] James Watt created

00:27:07.489 --> 00:27:10.358
the Mineral Management
Service to be responsible

00:27:10.358 --> 00:27:13.461
for the leasing of public
lands for exploration

00:27:13.461 --> 00:27:17.899
and collection of oil and gas
revenue for the government,

00:27:17.899 --> 00:27:21.736
as well as to ensure that
companies were operating lawfully

00:27:21.736 --> 00:27:24.072
and protecting the safety of their workers

00:27:24.072 --> 00:27:25.173
and the environment.

00:27:27.976 --> 00:27:32.347
The Gulf would be the main site
for deep water exploration,

00:27:32.347 --> 00:27:34.416
becoming what environmentalists termed

00:27:34.416 --> 00:27:37.252
a national sacrifice area.

00:27:38.887 --> 00:27:43.491
Today, 99% of the nation's
offshore oil production occurs

00:27:43.491 --> 00:27:44.693
in the Gulf.

00:27:47.295 --> 00:27:51.232
The MMS became a partner
to the oil industry.

00:27:51.232 --> 00:27:52.801
Because they were so interconnected,

00:27:52.801 --> 00:27:55.637
their regulatory authority disintegrated.

00:27:56.571 --> 00:28:00.508
MMS officials ignored
environmental reviews,

00:28:00.508 --> 00:28:04.345
were lenient in pursuing
companies over equipment failures,

00:28:04.345 --> 00:28:07.882
and abandoned proposals that
would have improved safety.

00:28:09.517 --> 00:28:11.853
The resulting imbalance of authority led

00:28:11.853 --> 00:28:15.156
to a culture conducive to corruption.

00:28:15.156 --> 00:28:19.761
- There's a concept in government
called regulatory capture,

00:28:19.761 --> 00:28:24.766
and that is that the regulating
agency works so closely

00:28:25.033 --> 00:28:28.536
with the industry, they deal
with each other every day,

00:28:28.536 --> 00:28:30.772
they start to know each other.

00:28:30.772 --> 00:28:33.141
Capture occurs when they deal with,

00:28:33.141 --> 00:28:35.343
when they become so comfortable,

00:28:35.343 --> 00:28:37.779
that it is the industry that dictates

00:28:37.779 --> 00:28:40.815
to the regulatory agency what happens

00:28:40.815 --> 00:28:42.617
rather than the other way around.

00:28:44.719 --> 00:28:46.855
A lot of people use the phrase

00:28:46.855 --> 00:28:51.392
that the regulatory agency
is in bed with the industry,

00:28:51.392 --> 00:28:53.228
and they use it as a metaphor,

00:28:53.228 --> 00:28:55.763
but you can literally say that happened

00:28:55.763 --> 00:28:57.465
with Mineral Management Service

00:28:57.465 --> 00:28:59.200
and some of the oil and gas companies

00:28:59.200 --> 00:29:02.303
that they were supposed to be overseeing.

00:29:03.204 --> 00:29:04.405
The Inspector General

00:29:04.405 --> 00:29:09.410
for inspecting the Mineral
Management Service in 2008 found

00:29:09.911 --> 00:29:14.182
some really egregious, some
R-rated violations of ethics.

00:29:18.786 --> 00:29:21.889
The best quote that I heard
was, "Well, if you're in bed

00:29:21.889 --> 00:29:23.691
"with the people you're
supposed to be regulating,

00:29:23.691 --> 00:29:25.326
you can't be at arms-length," which is...

00:29:25.326 --> 00:29:29.230
Pretty much--
- (chuckles) Sums it up.

00:29:29.230 --> 00:29:31.332
- Sums it up, yeah, absolutely.

00:29:32.633 --> 00:29:33.468
- [Narrator] The Office

00:29:33.468 --> 00:29:35.970
of the Inspector General gave
us a glimpse into this culture

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:39.340
through investigative reports
that tell startling stories

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:41.909
of the Minerals Management Service

00:29:41.909 --> 00:29:44.112
and its relationship
with the oil industry.

00:29:45.480 --> 00:29:49.884
One investigation found
Director Gregory W. Smith worked

00:29:49.884 --> 00:29:53.254
for an outside engineering
firm and used his role

00:29:53.254 --> 00:29:57.959
in the MMS to connect them
with industry clients.

00:29:57.959 --> 00:30:00.461
He often requested that
subordinates provide him

00:30:00.461 --> 00:30:03.364
with cocaine, crystal meth,

00:30:03.364 --> 00:30:05.767
and engage in sexual activities.

00:30:06.934 --> 00:30:08.536
Another investigation revealed

00:30:08.536 --> 00:30:11.506
that MMS employees had accepted gifts

00:30:11.506 --> 00:30:15.710
from the oil and gas industry
on numerous occasions.

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:19.780
These are direct violations of
government ethics standards,

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:21.482
and one representative admitted

00:30:21.482 --> 00:30:25.486
that business was never
conducted at such events.

00:30:29.223 --> 00:30:31.959
The investigations also found emails laden

00:30:31.959 --> 00:30:35.229
with sexual innuendoes from
industry representatives,

00:30:35.229 --> 00:30:37.932
inviting MMS officials to parties.

00:30:40.101 --> 00:30:42.904
There was also evidence
that employees took steps

00:30:42.904 --> 00:30:47.408
to keep their social contacts
with the industry a secret.

00:30:47.408 --> 00:30:50.811
The investigation uncovered
emails from MMS employees

00:30:50.811 --> 00:30:55.750
stating their intendance was
to be, quote, kept quiet,

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:59.020
and that they should RSVP in private.

00:31:01.756 --> 00:31:02.857
It was also proven

00:31:02.857 --> 00:31:07.862
that MMS employees frequently
consumed alcohol, cocaine,

00:31:08.029 --> 00:31:10.798
and marijuana at these functions,

00:31:10.798 --> 00:31:13.000
as well as had sexual relations

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:15.569
with oil industry representatives.

00:31:19.073 --> 00:31:22.543
- The Minerals Management Service
MMS Lake Charles District:

00:31:22.543 --> 00:31:25.246
"We're all oil industry."

00:31:25.246 --> 00:31:27.848
The report saying oil industry
officials would fill out

00:31:27.848 --> 00:31:31.419
safety inspection reports
themselves in pencil,

00:31:31.419 --> 00:31:35.089
and MMS inspectors would
then trace over them in pen

00:31:35.089 --> 00:31:36.757
before signing them.

00:31:36.757 --> 00:31:38.793
Then there was the inspector
who showed up for work,

00:31:38.793 --> 00:31:41.062
still high on crystal meth,

00:31:41.062 --> 00:31:44.165
and the other inspector who
inspected four offshore rigs

00:31:44.165 --> 00:31:47.168
of a company he was, at
the time, negotiating with

00:31:47.168 --> 00:31:49.437
for a job, which he got.

00:31:49.437 --> 00:31:51.639
The rigs, meanwhile, got no violations.

00:31:51.639 --> 00:31:52.773
And the other inspectors,

00:31:52.773 --> 00:31:54.508
who got free lunches from Big Oil,

00:31:54.508 --> 00:31:57.211
and other meals, too,
and gifts and golfing

00:31:57.211 --> 00:31:58.779
and shooting and hunting trips

00:31:58.779 --> 00:32:02.116
and tickets for the 2005 Peach Bowl.

00:32:02.116 --> 00:32:04.018
The bigger problem, perhaps not the gifts,

00:32:04.018 --> 00:32:06.220
but the suggestion they were unnecessary,

00:32:06.220 --> 00:32:09.323
and a revolving door agency
filled with childhood friends

00:32:09.323 --> 00:32:11.525
of employees of the oil industry.

00:32:11.525 --> 00:32:14.929
A ConocoPhillips employee,
emailing an MMS inspector,

00:32:14.929 --> 00:32:16.831
quote, "Ever get bribed?"

00:32:16.831 --> 00:32:18.399
"They try all the time."

00:32:18.399 --> 00:32:19.433
"Ever take 'em?"

00:32:19.433 --> 00:32:22.036
"I accept," quote, "gifts
from certain people.

00:32:22.036 --> 00:32:24.238
"But we have very strict ethics standards,

00:32:24.238 --> 00:32:25.372
"as you could imagine."

00:32:25.372 --> 00:32:28.242
"Certain people?" The
ConocoPhillips employee asks.

00:32:28.242 --> 00:32:29.543
"Meaning women?"

00:32:29.543 --> 00:32:31.478
"No," the MMS inspector writes.

00:32:31.478 --> 00:32:34.381
"Meaning good friends that
I wouldn't write up anyway."

00:32:34.381 --> 00:32:36.984
- MMS was asleep at the wheel,

00:32:36.984 --> 00:32:38.118
and that needs to be corrected.

00:32:38.118 --> 00:32:41.522
They were more of a lapdog
as opposed to a watchdog,

00:32:41.522 --> 00:32:43.924
and the fact that BP's plans were approved

00:32:43.924 --> 00:32:46.026
when they talked about walruses and seals.

00:32:46.026 --> 00:32:47.995
We don't have any walruses
and seals down here.

00:32:47.995 --> 00:32:50.364
So obviously either nobody read it,

00:32:50.364 --> 00:32:53.434
or somebody read it and didn't
even pay attention to it.

00:32:53.434 --> 00:32:56.103
It's probably worse that
they read it and approved it,

00:32:56.103 --> 00:32:58.872
You can understand maybe not reading it.

00:32:58.872 --> 00:33:00.708
- MMS adopted wholesale

00:33:00.708 --> 00:33:05.412
for its oil and drilling
best practices proposals

00:33:05.412 --> 00:33:08.082
of the American petroleum institute,

00:33:08.082 --> 00:33:08.983
and then they made most

00:33:08.983 --> 00:33:11.952
of those best practices only suggestions.

00:33:12.853 --> 00:33:14.355
There's been virtually no enforcement.

00:33:14.355 --> 00:33:18.025
According to the MMS website,
between 2000 and 2009,

00:33:18.025 --> 00:33:23.030
civil penalties averaged less
than $130 per well per year

00:33:24.865 --> 00:33:26.633
on our outer continental shelf,

00:33:26.633 --> 00:33:29.870
and only three criminal
referrals were made

00:33:29.870 --> 00:33:34.007
to the department since
1990, in the last 20 years.

00:33:35.542 --> 00:33:38.812
Added all up, and there
is no real question:

00:33:38.812 --> 00:33:41.515
MMS was a captive regulator.

00:33:43.684 --> 00:33:45.419
- Give 'em some penalties

00:33:46.653 --> 00:33:49.790
that will give them
incentive not to do this,

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:51.859
no matter how much money
they get under the table

00:33:51.859 --> 00:33:52.893
and all that kinda stuff.

00:33:52.893 --> 00:33:54.762
If they gonna go to prison
for the rest of their life

00:33:54.762 --> 00:33:56.196
for getting in bed with BP,

00:33:57.498 --> 00:34:00.100
you don't let 'em retire with their...

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:01.969
Replace 'em and let 'em retire.

00:34:03.203 --> 00:34:06.473
It's been peddled too softly,

00:34:06.473 --> 00:34:08.242
and they need some real penalties

00:34:08.242 --> 00:34:10.277
on the people who supposed
to be the watchdog.

00:34:10.277 --> 00:34:11.745
If they're not the watchdog,

00:34:11.745 --> 00:34:13.213
China takes 'em out and shoots 'em.

00:34:13.213 --> 00:34:16.083
I don't think we can
do that, but that's...

00:34:16.083 --> 00:34:18.485
You need to get serious with those people.

00:34:19.686 --> 00:34:22.923
- [Narrator] Finally,
everything was becoming clear.

00:34:22.923 --> 00:34:25.259
Deregulation and the government's faith

00:34:25.259 --> 00:34:27.895
in big industry spawned these types

00:34:27.895 --> 00:34:31.832
of incestuous relationships and
led the government officials

00:34:31.832 --> 00:34:35.836
to simply look the other
way while BP operated

00:34:35.836 --> 00:34:39.940
at the expense of the American
public and the environment.

00:34:41.708 --> 00:34:44.578
(pensive piano melody)

00:34:44.578 --> 00:34:48.448
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:34:48.448 --> 00:34:51.385
Although the MMS requires
some companies to identify

00:34:51.385 --> 00:34:55.622
what they can do to reduce a
project's environmental impact,

00:34:55.622 --> 00:34:58.625
the agency grants hundreds
of waivers each year.

00:34:59.660 --> 00:35:01.862
It was these types of federal waivers

00:35:01.862 --> 00:35:05.098
that essentially gave BP complete control

00:35:05.098 --> 00:35:07.467
over its drilling projects.

00:35:07.467 --> 00:35:11.071
In fact, just before the
Deepwater Horizon explosion,

00:35:11.071 --> 00:35:14.975
a senior director from BP
America wrote to the council,

00:35:14.975 --> 00:35:18.111
saying these exemptions
would help the industry

00:35:18.111 --> 00:35:21.915
avoid unnecessary
paperwork and time delays.

00:35:25.685 --> 00:35:27.587
Traveling further down the coast,

00:35:27.587 --> 00:35:30.524
more and more upset residents,
government officials,

00:35:30.524 --> 00:35:33.493
and specialists wanted to speak out,

00:35:33.493 --> 00:35:37.364
such as Billy Nungesser,
president of Plaquemines Parish.

00:35:37.364 --> 00:35:39.166
He is a staunch political force

00:35:39.166 --> 00:35:41.268
in Louisiana state government

00:35:41.268 --> 00:35:44.070
who had first-hand
experience dealing with BP

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:46.606
from the beginning of this disaster.

00:35:46.606 --> 00:35:50.043
He had discovered that BP's
only concern seemed to be

00:35:50.043 --> 00:35:53.780
protecting their bottom
line at almost any cost.

00:35:55.582 --> 00:35:58.118
- For 11 people who were
on a drilling platform

00:35:58.118 --> 00:36:01.021
when it exploded, sending
up a wall of fire.

00:36:01.021 --> 00:36:04.257
- The explosion sent six
people to USA Medical Center.

00:36:04.257 --> 00:36:06.893
One of them was sent to
the hospital's burn unit.

00:36:06.893 --> 00:36:09.829
- This rig is more than 50
miles southeast of Venice

00:36:09.829 --> 00:36:11.231
off Louisiana's tip.

00:36:11.231 --> 00:36:14.301
- I stood in Venice and listened
to the radio transmission

00:36:14.301 --> 00:36:16.336
as they pulled people out the water,

00:36:16.336 --> 00:36:17.871
and they kept those men

00:36:17.871 --> 00:36:20.740
on the back of that boat for 24 hours.

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:24.010
That's when you knew something
was wrong with this company,

00:36:24.010 --> 00:36:26.146
and it was being run
by a bunch of lawyers.

00:36:27.080 --> 00:36:28.748
Why'd they didn't bring
those people into Venice?

00:36:28.748 --> 00:36:30.684
I was standing by with our ambulances

00:36:30.684 --> 00:36:32.419
waiting to care for 'em.

00:36:32.419 --> 00:36:35.322
They kept 'em on a boat for 24 hours.

00:36:35.322 --> 00:36:36.923
That in itself is criminal.

00:36:37.857 --> 00:36:40.327
And it started from
there and went downhill.

00:36:43.363 --> 00:36:45.832
- And we read that oil
workers from the rig were held

00:36:45.832 --> 00:36:48.635
in seclusion on the open
water for up to two days

00:36:48.635 --> 00:36:51.438
after the April 20 explosion,

00:36:51.438 --> 00:36:53.406
while attorneys attempted to convince them

00:36:53.406 --> 00:36:55.575
to sign legal documents stating

00:36:55.575 --> 00:36:57.911
that they were unharmed by the incident,

00:36:57.911 --> 00:36:59.779
and then claimed that they were forbidden

00:36:59.779 --> 00:37:01.181
from having any contact

00:37:01.181 --> 00:37:03.483
with concerned loved ones during that time

00:37:03.483 --> 00:37:05.952
and were told that they
would not be able to go home

00:37:05.952 --> 00:37:10.590
until they signed the documents
they were presented with.

00:37:10.590 --> 00:37:13.259
After being awake for 50 harrowing hours,

00:37:13.259 --> 00:37:15.695
Stephen Davis caved in
and signed the papers.

00:37:15.695 --> 00:37:18.565
He said most of the others did as well.

00:37:18.565 --> 00:37:22.635
Do you think this is an
appropriate way to treat people

00:37:22.635 --> 00:37:24.771
that experience that?

00:37:24.771 --> 00:37:26.072
- Imagine what it would
have been like to be

00:37:26.072 --> 00:37:27.006
in that situation.

00:37:27.006 --> 00:37:30.410
So you eventually made it in
the lifeboat to a supply ship

00:37:30.410 --> 00:37:32.612
and then back to shore to a hotel,

00:37:32.612 --> 00:37:34.180
which is where you were asked

00:37:34.180 --> 00:37:36.316
by Transocean to sign this waiver

00:37:36.316 --> 00:37:38.485
that I have in my hand here,

00:37:38.485 --> 00:37:41.054
which says you were asked...

00:37:41.054 --> 00:37:44.390
I was not a witness to the
incident requiring the evacuation

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:45.558
and have had no firsthand

00:37:45.558 --> 00:37:47.660
or personal knowledge
regarding the incident.

00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:49.062
You put your signature to that.

00:37:49.062 --> 00:37:50.997
You were also asked if you were injured,

00:37:50.997 --> 00:37:53.199
and you said, "I was
not injured as a result

00:37:53.199 --> 00:37:55.335
"of the incident or the evacuation."

00:37:55.335 --> 00:37:58.605
Why did they say to you that
they wanted you to sign this?

00:38:00.206 --> 00:38:01.441
- They told me...

00:38:01.441 --> 00:38:02.275
Me and my wife were actually

00:38:02.275 --> 00:38:05.678
about to leave the hotel to come home.

00:38:05.678 --> 00:38:07.547
They caught me right before
I went out of the door.

00:38:07.547 --> 00:38:09.649
They told me it was
just a statement saying

00:38:09.649 --> 00:38:12.518
that I was off-tower,
that I wasn't working,

00:38:12.518 --> 00:38:15.922
and I didn't see what happened
leading up to the incident.

00:38:17.290 --> 00:38:19.158
Me and my wife were
just trying to get home

00:38:19.158 --> 00:38:20.560
to see the rest of our family,

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:22.428
and they said just sign here,

00:38:22.428 --> 00:38:24.330
initial here, initial here, sign this.

00:38:25.665 --> 00:38:27.500
- Tragedy of the 11 people out there,

00:38:27.500 --> 00:38:30.403
many of these people
came from our community.

00:38:31.504 --> 00:38:35.041
There was really a human tragedy there

00:38:35.041 --> 00:38:36.609
that probably wasn't felt as much

00:38:36.609 --> 00:38:38.945
in other parts of the country,

00:38:38.945 --> 00:38:40.179
but it was very much felt here.

00:38:40.179 --> 00:38:41.614
People knew these people

00:38:42.849 --> 00:38:45.051
and grieved with the families.

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:52.125
Initially, the well is
considered pretty far offshore.

00:38:53.059 --> 00:38:56.262
It was thought that, we thought,

00:38:56.262 --> 00:38:58.698
"Well, they'll get it under control."

00:38:58.698 --> 00:39:00.833
They had the blowout preventer on there.

00:39:02.535 --> 00:39:04.370
Our hopes were raised every day

00:39:04.370 --> 00:39:06.172
and then dashed and raised and dashed,

00:39:06.172 --> 00:39:08.274
and I don't know how
many times that happened.

00:39:08.274 --> 00:39:09.175
- [Reporter] For four days,

00:39:09.175 --> 00:39:11.611
the spill was thought
to have been contained.

00:39:11.611 --> 00:39:14.947
All the while, crude oil
was pumping into the Gulf.

00:39:14.947 --> 00:39:17.817
42,000 gallons a day are gushing out,

00:39:17.817 --> 00:39:19.952
but should the well itself open,

00:39:19.952 --> 00:39:23.156
hundred times more crude
could spew into the water.

00:39:23.156 --> 00:39:25.258
It could take vastly longer to contain

00:39:25.258 --> 00:39:26.592
than previously thought.

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:28.961
- My initial reaction was,

00:39:28.961 --> 00:39:31.731
I felt terrible for the
families of the people

00:39:31.731 --> 00:39:33.199
who lost their lives on the rig,

00:39:33.199 --> 00:39:34.433
and I thought nothing of the fact

00:39:34.433 --> 00:39:36.702
that, well, they might not
be able to plug this thing.

00:39:36.702 --> 00:39:39.639
I figured it'd be like any other disaster

00:39:39.639 --> 00:39:42.608
or any other accident
in that type industry.

00:39:42.608 --> 00:39:45.278
A week and a half, two weeks
go by and they plug it up,

00:39:45.278 --> 00:39:49.048
and boom it off around the
rig and that's the end of it.

00:39:49.048 --> 00:39:51.617
The funerals go on for the
lost lives of the workers,

00:39:51.617 --> 00:39:53.986
but I don't think anybody had any idea

00:39:56.455 --> 00:39:57.857
what that was gonna lead to.

00:39:59.659 --> 00:40:02.461
I was kinda the of the ones,
I stayed real positive.

00:40:02.461 --> 00:40:04.430
Once everybody started to kind of realize

00:40:04.430 --> 00:40:06.766
just how bad this could possibly be...

00:40:06.766 --> 00:40:07.700
I try to stay positive.

00:40:07.700 --> 00:40:09.302
That's the only way that
I can deal with things,

00:40:09.302 --> 00:40:11.504
and I was thinking, "Oh,
well, maybe it's not,

00:40:11.504 --> 00:40:14.607
"it's a natural occurring product,

00:40:14.607 --> 00:40:16.576
"so maybe it won't be that
toxic on the environment.

00:40:16.576 --> 00:40:17.777
"Maybe there's not near as much

00:40:17.777 --> 00:40:19.512
"as they're saying that's leaking."

00:40:20.413 --> 00:40:22.014
There's only so much that
you can tell yourself,

00:40:22.014 --> 00:40:23.616
and then you go out there
and see that stuff every day

00:40:23.616 --> 00:40:25.518
and just thinking, "God!"

00:40:25.518 --> 00:40:26.352
We go out there,

00:40:26.352 --> 00:40:29.889
and our boat looks like
we've been in a battlefield,

00:40:29.889 --> 00:40:32.325
stuff all over the place,

00:40:32.325 --> 00:40:34.961
nasty shovels and oil
oil all over the boat.

00:40:36.629 --> 00:40:38.964
After a day of doing that,

00:40:38.964 --> 00:40:42.335
you're just saying to
yourself, "This Gulf that I...

00:40:43.369 --> 00:40:46.906
"My way of life could possibly
be over for a long time."

00:40:46.906 --> 00:40:50.042
And that was the hardest
pill for me to swallow.

00:40:50.042 --> 00:40:53.446
I had a hard time, after
seeing how bad it really was,

00:40:53.446 --> 00:40:54.680
staying positive.

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:56.949
- There are many checks before you get

00:40:56.949 --> 00:41:00.553
to activating the blowout preventer.

00:41:00.553 --> 00:41:03.289
That is the ultimate failsafe mechanism,

00:41:03.289 --> 00:41:06.225
and for whatever reason, and
we don't understand that yet,

00:41:06.225 --> 00:41:08.661
but we clearly will as a consequence

00:41:08.661 --> 00:41:12.398
of both our investigation
and federal investigations,

00:41:12.398 --> 00:41:13.966
it failed to operate.

00:41:13.966 --> 00:41:15.868
- In November of last year,

00:41:15.868 --> 00:41:18.237
Transocean pulled out the blowup preventer

00:41:18.237 --> 00:41:21.841
from the ocean floor because
its shear rams weren't working,

00:41:21.841 --> 00:41:24.243
the lower annular would not close

00:41:24.243 --> 00:41:27.046
and the upper annular had been stripped

00:41:28.781 --> 00:41:31.150
during a well control event.

00:41:31.150 --> 00:41:33.586
- This is an annular blowout preventer.

00:41:33.586 --> 00:41:35.287
It's actually a 20-inch bore.

00:41:35.287 --> 00:41:38.457
The ones used on the
Horizon were higher pressure

00:41:38.457 --> 00:41:40.860
and 18 3/4 inch bore,

00:41:40.860 --> 00:41:43.229
but there woulda been
two roughly this size

00:41:43.229 --> 00:41:44.864
in the blowout preventer stack.

00:41:46.265 --> 00:41:49.368
These were primarily the primary,

00:41:49.368 --> 00:41:54.373
the main devices used to
actually seal off the wellbore,

00:41:55.341 --> 00:41:58.344
either to prevent a
kick or during testing.

00:41:58.344 --> 00:42:00.713
And they basically have a rubber elastomer

00:42:00.713 --> 00:42:04.049
that can expand and contract
with hydraulic pressure

00:42:04.049 --> 00:42:06.452
to seal off the wellbore.

00:42:06.452 --> 00:42:09.989
- We know that BP was aware
of this because Transocean,

00:42:09.989 --> 00:42:12.625
and again it's document
number 12 there in our binder,

00:42:12.625 --> 00:42:14.693
reflect conference calls with BP

00:42:14.693 --> 00:42:16.562
about the problems with
this blowout preventer.

00:42:16.562 --> 00:42:17.529
- Just before he lands,

00:42:17.529 --> 00:42:21.166
he realizes he has not
engaged his landing gear.

00:42:21.166 --> 00:42:22.968
So he puts the landing gear,

00:42:22.968 --> 00:42:25.704
and the landing gear
doesn't get down in time,

00:42:25.704 --> 00:42:27.373
and the plane crashes.

00:42:27.373 --> 00:42:28.707
So we have an investigation

00:42:28.707 --> 00:42:31.644
on how do we get better landing gears,

00:42:31.644 --> 00:42:33.979
and to me, that's what we have here.

00:42:33.979 --> 00:42:36.849
We're looking at the
wrong thing, the blowout.

00:42:36.849 --> 00:42:38.350
We're spending too much time looking

00:42:38.350 --> 00:42:39.318
at the blowout preventers,

00:42:39.318 --> 00:42:41.754
and the blowout preventers
are the landing gear.

00:42:41.754 --> 00:42:45.090
And what we should be
focusing on is the procedures

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:46.325
that were done.

00:42:46.325 --> 00:42:47.960
- As you can see, your engineers said

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:50.696
that if you used a long
string of casing, quote,

00:42:50.696 --> 00:42:55.167
"it is unlikely to be a
successful cement job.

00:42:55.167 --> 00:42:58.771
"You would be unable to
fulfill MMS regulations,

00:42:58.771 --> 00:43:02.007
"and there would be an open
annulus to the wellhead."

00:43:02.007 --> 00:43:03.675
I have that on the screen.

00:43:03.675 --> 00:43:05.644
Now those are serious risks!

00:43:05.644 --> 00:43:10.082
A failed cement job, a violation
of MMS safety regulations,

00:43:10.082 --> 00:43:15.087
an open pathway for gas to
travel to the top of the well.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:17.956
The same document says
that if you use the liner

00:43:17.956 --> 00:43:21.360
and tieback approach,
which is what ExxonMobil

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:24.029
and other companies said
you should have used,

00:43:24.029 --> 00:43:27.065
you would have avoided
or lessened these risks.

00:43:27.065 --> 00:43:29.401
Yet BP apparently overruled the warnings

00:43:29.401 --> 00:43:34.206
of its own engineers and chose
the more dangerous option.

00:43:34.206 --> 00:43:37.309
How can you explain that decision by BP?

00:43:37.309 --> 00:43:39.077
Why were the safety recommendations

00:43:39.077 --> 00:43:42.247
of your own engineers ignored?

00:43:42.247 --> 00:43:44.750
- I wasn't involved in any
of the decision making.

00:43:45.751 --> 00:43:46.585
- To have a blowout,

00:43:46.585 --> 00:43:49.555
you have to have a
sequential type of errors,

00:43:49.555 --> 00:43:51.223
and what the first thing was,

00:43:51.223 --> 00:43:54.259
is that they ran this long
string of pipe in there.

00:43:55.994 --> 00:43:58.563
Number two was the
installation of that pipe

00:43:58.563 --> 00:44:02.200
and the procedures they used were wrong.

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:05.437
From what I understand,
they were in a hurry,

00:44:05.437 --> 00:44:06.338
and they did not wait

00:44:06.338 --> 00:44:08.540
for additional centralizers to get there.

00:44:08.540 --> 00:44:11.176
- So the experts said you need 21.

00:44:11.176 --> 00:44:13.312
Then, and if we can
put up the first slide,

00:44:13.312 --> 00:44:16.381
a BP employee essentially
wrote to that expert

00:44:16.381 --> 00:44:18.650
and said, "Well, we've only got six,

00:44:18.650 --> 00:44:21.253
"and we don't have time to
deal with this problem."

00:44:22.354 --> 00:44:24.956
Time to British Petroleum was money.

00:44:24.956 --> 00:44:29.928
This rig was 45 days late,
it cost you $500,000 a day,

00:44:30.162 --> 00:44:32.564
and people's obvious
attention were about time,

00:44:32.564 --> 00:44:34.132
which meant money.

00:44:34.132 --> 00:44:35.367
So what happened then?

00:44:36.368 --> 00:44:39.738
Well, another British
Petroleum person sent a memo,

00:44:39.738 --> 00:44:42.674
saying, "We really need
to follow the model here."

00:44:42.674 --> 00:44:44.843
He kicked it up to Mr. Guide.

00:44:44.843 --> 00:44:47.179
If we could have the second slide.

00:44:47.179 --> 00:44:48.547
Mr. Guide came back and said,

00:44:48.547 --> 00:44:52.150
"I don't like the fact the is
gonna take 10 hours to do."

00:44:52.150 --> 00:44:54.453
Even though another British
Petroleum person had said,

00:44:54.453 --> 00:44:56.021
"We're gonna fly 15 things in.

00:44:56.021 --> 00:44:58.657
"They could be here tomorrow morning."

00:44:58.657 --> 00:45:02.594
Mr. Guide said, "I don't
like the 10 hours,"

00:45:02.594 --> 00:45:03.962
and it didn't happen.

00:45:04.796 --> 00:45:08.066
- So what we have is a number of things:

00:45:08.066 --> 00:45:09.568
They didn't centralize it,

00:45:09.568 --> 00:45:10.936
they didn't circulate long enough,

00:45:10.936 --> 00:45:15.941
they ran wrong type of float equipment,

00:45:17.342 --> 00:45:19.978
they didn't have enough cement volume,

00:45:22.814 --> 00:45:25.550
and the type of cement they
used may not have been correct.

00:45:25.550 --> 00:45:27.953
So you have a number of things there.

00:45:27.953 --> 00:45:30.622
- Is there any other
well, to your knowledge,

00:45:30.622 --> 00:45:35.627
in the Gulf of Mexico that has been done

00:45:35.827 --> 00:45:39.931
in the same manner as this
well that was being drilled

00:45:39.931 --> 00:45:41.900
under the Deepwater Horizon?

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:43.768
- There are many wells
in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:45:43.768 --> 00:45:45.270
- Are there any other wells

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:47.239
where you haven't put the proper
number of centralizers in?

00:45:47.239 --> 00:45:49.241
- There are many wells
in the Gulf of Mexico

00:45:49.241 --> 00:45:51.209
that have the same casing design.

00:45:51.209 --> 00:45:52.877
There are many wells
that have been drilled

00:45:52.877 --> 00:45:55.313
where the same cement procedure has been--

00:45:55.313 --> 00:45:57.949
- Now have the Minerals and
Management Service people

00:45:57.949 --> 00:46:01.319
been there and looked over
those with a fine tooth comb?

00:46:02.220 --> 00:46:06.524
- Everything that we do is
subject to regulatory oversight.

00:46:06.524 --> 00:46:09.260
- Are you changing your
procedures at those wells

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:10.829
as a result of things
that you've encountered

00:46:10.829 --> 00:46:12.497
in your investigation with the Deepwater--

00:46:12.497 --> 00:46:14.766
- As we learn, apologies.

00:46:14.766 --> 00:46:17.302
As we learn from our investigation,

00:46:17.302 --> 00:46:19.938
we will make appropriate changes,

00:46:21.406 --> 00:46:23.475
as I've already indicated.

00:46:23.475 --> 00:46:26.511
- The potential devastation
to the Gulf Coast,

00:46:26.511 --> 00:46:29.647
its economy and its people,

00:46:29.647 --> 00:46:32.217
require us to continue
our relentless efforts

00:46:32.217 --> 00:46:34.219
to stop the leak and contain the damage.

00:46:35.153 --> 00:46:37.288
There's already been a loss of life,

00:46:38.123 --> 00:46:42.260
damage to our coastline,
to fish and wildlife,

00:46:42.260 --> 00:46:44.229
and to the livelihoods of everyone

00:46:44.229 --> 00:46:47.499
from fishermen to
restaurant and hotel owners.

00:46:47.499 --> 00:46:51.069
(typewriter keys clicking)

00:46:51.069 --> 00:46:53.371
- The fact that they
had even less confidence

00:46:53.371 --> 00:46:55.840
in where this oil was heading told us

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:57.408
that we need to have a sense of urgency,

00:46:57.408 --> 00:47:00.345
and we need to start working
to protect ourselves.

00:47:00.345 --> 00:47:02.714
We had our first impact on May 12th,

00:47:02.714 --> 00:47:04.249
three days after declaring emergency

00:47:04.249 --> 00:47:05.850
with tarballs hitting the beach,

00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:09.120
and then seven days later we
had our first major impact,

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:12.123
with heavy brown crude
oil ending up, basically,

00:47:12.123 --> 00:47:14.492
scattered most of the 12 miles of beach

00:47:14.492 --> 00:47:17.328
that we have here in Fourchon Beach, so.

00:47:17.328 --> 00:47:20.365
- It was so unbelievably different
from one day to the next.

00:47:20.365 --> 00:47:21.332
It was just a joke.

00:47:21.332 --> 00:47:22.767
We had different bosses every day.

00:47:22.767 --> 00:47:24.402
We had different people
we were answering to.

00:47:24.402 --> 00:47:27.038
We had different partner boats,

00:47:27.038 --> 00:47:28.473
different people on our taskforce.

00:47:28.473 --> 00:47:30.842
We would just get trained
at doing something

00:47:30.842 --> 00:47:33.278
and felt like we were
like, "OK, we're trained.

00:47:33.278 --> 00:47:34.779
"We're adequate to do this job,"

00:47:34.779 --> 00:47:36.814
and then they'd tells us
to go do something else.

00:47:36.814 --> 00:47:38.750
Tell us, "Hey, why don't
you go check on that boom."

00:47:38.750 --> 00:47:40.184
"We're getting pretty good
at picking up this oil.

00:47:40.184 --> 00:47:41.185
"Why don't you let us keep doing this?

00:47:41.185 --> 00:47:42.420
"We'll keep out waters clean."

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:44.188
"No, we need you over
here checking this boom,"

00:47:44.188 --> 00:47:45.790
after 67 other boats
have already checked it.

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:49.193
So it was like that every
day, I mean, then...

00:47:49.193 --> 00:47:51.129
Oh, I could go on about getting paid,

00:47:51.129 --> 00:47:53.097
and "Oh, you're gonna
get paid within a week

00:47:53.097 --> 00:47:54.232
"after submitting your invoice."

00:47:54.232 --> 00:47:55.300
Yeah, my ass.

00:47:56.234 --> 00:47:59.137
The whole thing was just a debacle.

00:47:59.137 --> 00:47:59.971
- BP came in here,

00:47:59.971 --> 00:48:01.773
and they put me out of
the shrimp business,

00:48:01.773 --> 00:48:02.607
they put me out of the coast business,

00:48:02.607 --> 00:48:05.176
they put me out of the retail business

00:48:05.176 --> 00:48:07.011
'cause they took our tourists away.

00:48:07.011 --> 00:48:08.579
They put us out of the ice business

00:48:08.579 --> 00:48:11.983
because we make 130 tons of ice a day,

00:48:11.983 --> 00:48:14.585
and if the shrimp ain't
running, they don't need no ice.

00:48:14.585 --> 00:48:18.089
And the only thing we had
left was the fuel business,

00:48:18.089 --> 00:48:23.094
and BP brought in outsiders
from Texas and parked a barge.

00:48:23.294 --> 00:48:26.197
They don't pay no taxes over
here, don't pay nothing.

00:48:27.165 --> 00:48:30.868
I mean, I asked them if they
wanted my dog and my wife, too.

00:48:30.868 --> 00:48:32.837
You might as well take everything I got.

00:48:33.971 --> 00:48:35.339
- The oil showed up.

00:48:37.742 --> 00:48:39.043
I can't fish right now.

00:48:41.412 --> 00:48:45.416
There's a saying that I do what
I do best, and that's fish.

00:48:45.416 --> 00:48:47.218
Do what you do best and pay for the rest.

00:48:47.218 --> 00:48:49.587
A good friend of mine
told me that years ago,

00:48:49.587 --> 00:48:51.823
and it's kinda stuck in my head.

00:48:53.591 --> 00:48:56.627
That's what I do, I am
a charter fisherman.

00:48:56.627 --> 00:49:01.532
- This is what you call not
a disaster, this is a...

00:49:01.532 --> 00:49:02.366
I don't know what you want to call it,

00:49:02.366 --> 00:49:05.436
but this is the worst
that could ever happen,

00:49:05.436 --> 00:49:06.470
and it's not just us.

00:49:09.073 --> 00:49:12.476
Hey, we got people from
Mississippi, Alabama.

00:49:12.476 --> 00:49:14.312
Florida, Florida, we have a
lot of people from Florida

00:49:14.312 --> 00:49:17.882
that come fish off a here,
king mackerel and snapper,

00:49:17.882 --> 00:49:19.283
that fishes here, too.

00:49:19.283 --> 00:49:21.686
Mississippi comes over here to shrimp.

00:49:21.686 --> 00:49:23.921
So, and it's not just Louisiana
that's gonna be affected.

00:49:23.921 --> 00:49:25.056
We have people from Texas come here

00:49:25.056 --> 00:49:26.390
just to work these areas.

00:49:27.959 --> 00:49:29.560
It's everybody that's gonna be affected

00:49:29.560 --> 00:49:31.495
with this oil spill, everybody.

00:49:34.799 --> 00:49:37.802
- The oil spill hit at
the absolute worst time

00:49:37.802 --> 00:49:39.403
for our community.

00:49:39.403 --> 00:49:41.606
The businesses we have here are all local,

00:49:41.606 --> 00:49:42.540
family-owned businesses.

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:45.610
We don't have very many,
if any, big corporations

00:49:45.610 --> 00:49:47.945
that are set up in town operating.

00:49:47.945 --> 00:49:52.450
And so they, really, throughout
the summer bank their money.

00:49:52.450 --> 00:49:54.018
That's when they earn the money.

00:49:54.018 --> 00:49:55.353
There's about a hundred days of summer.

00:49:55.353 --> 00:49:57.822
We try to sneak through the
fall and over the winter

00:49:57.822 --> 00:50:00.758
and kinda get by until spring break.

00:50:00.758 --> 00:50:04.028
This year, 2010 spring break
season, was a breakout year.

00:50:04.028 --> 00:50:08.566
We had good growth, we had
8% or 9% growth in tourism.

00:50:08.566 --> 00:50:10.501
We had good money flowing in.

00:50:11.402 --> 00:50:14.038
People had, after the
different hurricane seasons

00:50:14.038 --> 00:50:16.340
that we had had and had some
rebuilding and some growth.

00:50:16.340 --> 00:50:18.275
Then we had a downturn in the economy.

00:50:18.275 --> 00:50:19.810
All of a sudden, this
seemed to be the year

00:50:19.810 --> 00:50:21.178
where it was up-ticking.

00:50:21.178 --> 00:50:25.182
And so, before the season
started, which is really,

00:50:25.182 --> 00:50:28.586
our Memorial Day starts our summer season.

00:50:28.586 --> 00:50:31.622
Before it got here and ever got started,

00:50:31.622 --> 00:50:35.392
we had the brakes put on and
struggled all summer long,

00:50:35.392 --> 00:50:37.628
and now I'm hoping that
the businesses can survive

00:50:37.628 --> 00:50:40.331
through the winter, through
another down period,

00:50:40.331 --> 00:50:41.699
and be prepared in springtime.

00:50:41.699 --> 00:50:44.702
So we're fighting not
necessarily for 2010 anymore.

00:50:44.702 --> 00:50:49.373
Now we're fighting to survive
to get to 2011, next year,

00:50:49.373 --> 00:50:51.675
to make sure that the businesses

00:50:51.675 --> 00:50:54.778
that are here can be here next year.

00:50:54.778 --> 00:50:58.449
- There was double-digit
decline in business, some 20%.

00:50:58.449 --> 00:51:01.519
Some of the real estate
folks were down 30%.

00:51:01.519 --> 00:51:03.921
These businesses in Pensacola thrive

00:51:05.155 --> 00:51:09.426
on a June, July, August vacation period.

00:51:09.426 --> 00:51:10.261
That's when they make their money.

00:51:10.261 --> 00:51:11.462
That's when they save their money

00:51:11.462 --> 00:51:13.497
to make it through the rest of the year.

00:51:13.497 --> 00:51:16.967
And that business was down appreciably,

00:51:16.967 --> 00:51:18.569
and there were some
businesses that were saying,

00:51:18.569 --> 00:51:21.739
"We don't know if we can make it."

00:51:21.739 --> 00:51:24.008
- It bothers me that they'll
hire people from out of town,

00:51:24.008 --> 00:51:27.111
out of state and won't hire me.

00:51:27.111 --> 00:51:29.046
This is my living, this is my way of life.

00:51:29.046 --> 00:51:30.714
I want it back.

00:51:30.714 --> 00:51:32.449
(exciting music)

00:51:32.449 --> 00:51:33.350
- Larry, thanks very much.

00:51:33.350 --> 00:51:35.819
Breaking news tonight,
potentially chilling news.

00:51:35.819 --> 00:51:38.055
A leading expert, who you'll
hear from in a minute,

00:51:38.055 --> 00:51:40.658
has examined this video of oil gushing

00:51:40.658 --> 00:51:42.026
into the Gulf of Mexico,

00:51:42.026 --> 00:51:44.128
and what he says is horrifying.

00:51:44.128 --> 00:51:45.963
The leak, which the Coast Guard estimates

00:51:45.963 --> 00:51:50.968
at 5000 barrels a day, could
be far worse, 10 times worse,

00:51:50.968 --> 00:51:53.537
and that's the bottom end
of his estimated range,

00:51:53.537 --> 00:51:55.839
which means that this
expert is talking about

00:51:55.839 --> 00:51:57.808
at a bare minimum, about the equivalent

00:51:57.808 --> 00:52:01.145
of one Exxon Valdez spill every four days.

00:52:01.145 --> 00:52:03.247
- I think they lied
about the amount of oil

00:52:03.247 --> 00:52:05.416
that was going into the Gulf immediately.

00:52:05.416 --> 00:52:08.385
I will say that: L-I-E, lied!

00:52:09.586 --> 00:52:12.289
They knew that more oil
was going into the Gulf

00:52:12.289 --> 00:52:15.659
than they first admitted, OK?

00:52:15.659 --> 00:52:19.963
Their efforts to cap the well,
their efforts to do this.

00:52:19.963 --> 00:52:23.567
Whatever, that's all fine and dandy.

00:52:28.572 --> 00:52:30.641
All in all, I wasn't very happy with BP.

00:52:30.641 --> 00:52:32.843
- And there's an area
called Barataria Bay,

00:52:32.843 --> 00:52:35.145
and I would suggest you go look it up.

00:52:35.145 --> 00:52:36.380
About a week and a half ago,

00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:38.649
there was no oil in Barataria Bay.

00:52:38.649 --> 00:52:41.752
That section was scheduled to
be covered by the barrier plan

00:52:41.752 --> 00:52:43.654
that still to this day
hasn't been approved.

00:52:43.654 --> 00:52:47.991
Now today, there's oil, thick
oil coming into Barataria Bay.

00:52:47.991 --> 00:52:50.294
And so you're not showing
the sense of urgency,

00:52:50.294 --> 00:52:52.329
and whether it's you or
the Federal Government,

00:52:52.329 --> 00:52:55.299
we've got oil in Barataria
Bay when we had a plan

00:52:55.299 --> 00:52:58.602
a month ago to keep the
oil out a Barataria Bay.

00:52:58.602 --> 00:53:01.471
And so when people are hearing
that everything's being done,

00:53:01.471 --> 00:53:04.708
I'm a tell ya, on the ground,
it's not getting done!

00:53:04.708 --> 00:53:07.077
And I don't know what you
need to do differently,

00:53:07.077 --> 00:53:09.546
but you need to go do
something differently.

00:53:09.546 --> 00:53:11.348
And if it's not you that's blocking it,

00:53:11.348 --> 00:53:13.016
you need to tell somebody
who's blocking it

00:53:13.016 --> 00:53:14.251
because it's being blocked,

00:53:14.251 --> 00:53:16.219
and it's not getting done on the ground,

00:53:16.219 --> 00:53:18.689
and we don't have the luxury of time.

00:53:18.689 --> 00:53:19.890
This shouldn't be happening.

00:53:19.890 --> 00:53:22.926
- After the storm, they were
bringing boom out to safety.

00:53:22.926 --> 00:53:26.330
Then last night, they were
bringing the same boom out

00:53:26.330 --> 00:53:29.533
because it was the defective Chinese boom.

00:53:29.533 --> 00:53:31.101
So what is it?
- What?

00:53:31.101 --> 00:53:32.436
- We were lied to.

00:53:32.436 --> 00:53:34.504
We continue to be lied to,

00:53:34.504 --> 00:53:36.974
and I told the second commander

00:53:36.974 --> 00:53:39.142
who I just came from meeting with,

00:53:39.142 --> 00:53:42.479
I just told her everything,
I don't hold back.

00:53:42.479 --> 00:53:45.015
And she says, "Well, I
don't expect you to trust me

00:53:45.949 --> 00:53:48.352
"by saying it, I need
to show you by action."

00:53:48.352 --> 00:53:50.120
I said, "You absolutely do."

00:53:50.120 --> 00:53:52.789
And I said, "From this moment
on, you have an opportunity.

00:53:52.789 --> 00:53:54.157
"Here's my phone numbers.

00:53:54.157 --> 00:53:56.760
"For any equipment moves in this parish,

00:53:56.760 --> 00:54:00.464
"I deserve a phone call,
so there's your chance."

00:54:00.464 --> 00:54:03.066
- After it became obvious we
were gonna have to deal with it

00:54:03.066 --> 00:54:06.169
then the focus and the working
relationship with BP was,

00:54:06.169 --> 00:54:07.437
how do we deal with it?

00:54:07.437 --> 00:54:08.338
How do we clean it up?

00:54:08.338 --> 00:54:10.807
How do we get it skimmed
before it gets here?

00:54:10.807 --> 00:54:13.276
How do we protect our backwater areas?

00:54:13.276 --> 00:54:15.212
You know, that kind of
working relationship,

00:54:15.212 --> 00:54:17.848
which started off pretty rocky

00:54:17.848 --> 00:54:20.784
because they had transitioning
people in and out

00:54:20.784 --> 00:54:23.320
before they ever have anybody
permanently located here.

00:54:23.320 --> 00:54:25.322
And we were dealing with one guy one week,

00:54:25.322 --> 00:54:28.024
and they'd take a two
week reprieve and be gone,

00:54:28.024 --> 00:54:29.359
and we'd be dealing with somebody else,

00:54:29.359 --> 00:54:31.127
and there was no consistency.

00:54:31.127 --> 00:54:33.864
- I felt like we were
completely in the dark.

00:54:33.864 --> 00:54:35.799
No one knew anything.

00:54:35.799 --> 00:54:37.634
Scientists didn't know anything.

00:54:37.634 --> 00:54:40.437
You couldn't get any
information out of anyone

00:54:40.437 --> 00:54:42.138
that you thought should know something.

00:54:42.138 --> 00:54:44.374
I mean, I worked at Hopedale

00:54:44.374 --> 00:54:48.278
when they were just setting
up the volunteer system,

00:54:48.278 --> 00:54:51.481
and no one there knew
anything that was going on.

00:54:51.481 --> 00:54:54.351
I mean, you'd walk to the
Fish and Wildlife trailer,

00:54:54.351 --> 00:54:56.920
and they didn't know what was going on.

00:54:56.920 --> 00:55:00.891
It just really was blank, it was a blank.

00:55:00.891 --> 00:55:02.259
- And I said, "Houma.

00:55:02.259 --> 00:55:04.628
"Still don't know who I call at Houma.

00:55:04.628 --> 00:55:06.096
"The Wizard of Oz,
(interviewer chuckles)

00:55:06.096 --> 00:55:08.398
"and some man hiding behind a curtain."

00:55:08.398 --> 00:55:11.067
There's nobody, they still
couldn't give me a name.

00:55:11.067 --> 00:55:12.769
"Well, I'll find out who in Houma."

00:55:12.769 --> 00:55:14.571
Say, "Well, when you do, let me know,

00:55:14.571 --> 00:55:18.208
"'cause we'll travel that
yellow brick road together."

00:55:18.208 --> 00:55:21.378
Because I can't but for the life of me see

00:55:21.378 --> 00:55:24.247
how a town a Houma can
make so many decisions

00:55:24.247 --> 00:55:26.149
without having a name behind it.

00:55:26.149 --> 00:55:28.618
- Communications were
not what they should be.

00:55:28.618 --> 00:55:32.188
I think everybody would agree to that.

00:55:32.188 --> 00:55:34.924
The Unified Command, of
course, was over in Mobile.

00:55:34.924 --> 00:55:36.860
And what our premise was,

00:55:36.860 --> 00:55:39.329
that we in Pensacola knew our beaches,

00:55:39.329 --> 00:55:41.097
we knew our bays, we knew our bayous,

00:55:41.097 --> 00:55:42.866
we knew how to take care of them.

00:55:42.866 --> 00:55:45.235
Let us have more control.

00:55:45.235 --> 00:55:49.272
But all the control, all the
requests had to go through

00:55:49.272 --> 00:55:51.408
that particular chain of command,

00:55:51.408 --> 00:55:54.577
and it took a long time, and
it was very, very frustrating

00:55:54.577 --> 00:55:56.079
because as I said before,

00:55:56.913 --> 00:55:59.149
we knew we had this horrible problem,

00:55:59.149 --> 00:56:01.818
but there were so many questions
as to how to combat it.

00:56:01.818 --> 00:56:03.353
It was a huge unknown.

00:56:03.353 --> 00:56:05.088
- With BP out of the way, it
would raise the question of:

00:56:05.088 --> 00:56:06.756
Replace them with what?

00:56:06.756 --> 00:56:08.958
- [Reporter] Only BP, he
said, has the equipment

00:56:08.958 --> 00:56:12.295
and expertise to plug a leak
a mile below the surface.

00:56:12.295 --> 00:56:14.230
The government does not.

00:56:14.230 --> 00:56:16.499
And he said he's still confident BP,

00:56:16.499 --> 00:56:18.468
with aggressive pressure
from the government,

00:56:18.468 --> 00:56:19.769
will get the job done.

00:56:19.769 --> 00:56:22.172
- You know what Allen told me yesterday?

00:56:22.172 --> 00:56:24.340
"I'm 51% in charge."

00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:25.875
Are you a little pregnant?

00:56:25.875 --> 00:56:27.310
Do you kiss your sister?

00:56:27.310 --> 00:56:30.980
I mean, what's 51% in charge?

00:56:32.649 --> 00:56:35.185
Either you're in, what
kinda leader tells you,

00:56:35.185 --> 00:56:37.687
"Hey, look, we're going
into war, but I'm 51,

00:56:37.687 --> 00:56:42.192
"if you get shot, that's on
the 49% that ain't in charge."

00:56:42.192 --> 00:56:43.893
If we win this...

00:56:43.893 --> 00:56:47.196
I mean, how do you tell the troops,

00:56:47.196 --> 00:56:50.400
"Hey, guys, let's go fight
this war, I'm 51% in charge."

00:56:50.400 --> 00:56:52.835
It's a business deal.

00:56:52.835 --> 00:56:55.538
- Now we are operating six
remote-operated vehicles

00:56:55.538 --> 00:56:57.907
simultaneously around
the blowout preventer,

00:56:57.907 --> 00:57:00.610
both monitoring the blowout preventer

00:57:00.610 --> 00:57:04.314
and trying to close in the flow of oil.

00:57:04.314 --> 00:57:06.849
Development driller
three is now on location,

00:57:06.849 --> 00:57:08.918
and we expect to start
drilling the relief well

00:57:08.918 --> 00:57:10.353
within 48 hours.

00:57:10.353 --> 00:57:14.157
Also, a new technique
identified yesterday afternoon,

00:57:14.157 --> 00:57:18.494
which is to try to deploy
dispersants at the source,

00:57:18.494 --> 00:57:20.296
at the location subsea.

00:57:23.299 --> 00:57:24.467
- Well, what they told us was,

00:57:24.467 --> 00:57:26.135
the dispersants would break it up

00:57:26.135 --> 00:57:29.005
and make it sink to the bottom
and it wouldn't come ashore,

00:57:29.005 --> 00:57:32.141
that I was being paranoid, and
it wasn't gonna come ashore.

00:57:32.141 --> 00:57:34.410
It was gonna stay off shore.

00:57:34.410 --> 00:57:37.513
And three weeks and four
days from that time,

00:57:37.513 --> 00:57:40.349
Pass a Loutre got inundated with oil.

00:57:40.349 --> 00:57:44.454
And I went back out five days in a row.

00:57:44.454 --> 00:57:45.421
Then on the seventh day,

00:57:45.421 --> 00:57:49.125
I brought Anderson Cooper
and the governor out there,

00:57:49.125 --> 00:57:52.528
and a week later, I brought
Anderson Cooper back,

00:57:52.528 --> 00:57:54.363
and still nobody did anything,

00:57:54.363 --> 00:57:56.065
and then the third week, still.

00:57:56.999 --> 00:57:59.068
It was almost a month
that oil sat out there

00:57:59.068 --> 00:58:01.170
with birds dying in the oil.

00:58:01.170 --> 00:58:03.573
All the fish in that area died.

00:58:04.640 --> 00:58:06.909
There was no life in that marsh,

00:58:06.909 --> 00:58:08.110
and that marsh is dead.

00:58:09.345 --> 00:58:10.947
It'll wash away,

00:58:10.947 --> 00:58:14.617
and South Louisiana will start
about a half mile inland.

00:58:15.618 --> 00:58:17.320
That marsh will be gone next year.

00:58:18.421 --> 00:58:21.290
- In the Dictionary.com
definition of disperse,

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:24.760
one, to drive or send off
in various directions,

00:58:24.760 --> 00:58:25.695
to scatter.

00:58:26.929 --> 00:58:30.533
Definition two, to spread
widely, disseminate,

00:58:30.533 --> 00:58:32.201
to dispel, cause to vanish.

00:58:32.201 --> 00:58:33.936
So really what they're doing,

00:58:33.936 --> 00:58:35.171
if you get to the core of this,

00:58:35.171 --> 00:58:37.240
and keep me honest on this.

00:58:37.240 --> 00:58:40.142
But really what they're doing
is making us not be able

00:58:40.142 --> 00:58:43.412
to see the disaster that is in our gulf.

00:58:43.412 --> 00:58:44.780
They're not fixing anything.

00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:46.315
We just can't see it anymore.

00:58:46.315 --> 00:58:47.516
- The difference here is

00:58:47.516 --> 00:58:50.186
that we have put these surfactants out

00:58:50.186 --> 00:58:52.822
at unprecedented quantities.

00:58:52.822 --> 00:58:54.957
It's an open ocean spill.

00:58:54.957 --> 00:58:57.059
The other unprecedented part of this is

00:58:57.059 --> 00:58:58.594
that we've put surfactants

00:58:58.594 --> 00:59:02.131
at the wellhead a mile down in the water,

00:59:02.131 --> 00:59:05.935
so we have dispersed oil
through the entire water column.

00:59:05.935 --> 00:59:06.902
Now what was misleading

00:59:06.902 --> 00:59:08.604
about what EPA has been coming out with,

00:59:08.604 --> 00:59:11.974
they said that oil and
oil plus dispersants,

00:59:11.974 --> 00:59:14.310
there's no difference in
toxicity of these two.

00:59:15.378 --> 00:59:17.213
The difference that they're glossing over,

00:59:17.213 --> 00:59:18.881
that's technically true,

00:59:18.881 --> 00:59:22.685
that oil and oil plus dispersants
have the same toxicity,

00:59:22.685 --> 00:59:25.688
or similar toxicity on a concentration

00:59:25.688 --> 00:59:27.890
per concentration basis, right?

00:59:27.890 --> 00:59:29.425
They have similar toxicity.

00:59:29.425 --> 00:59:31.427
The difference is, the bioavailability,

00:59:31.427 --> 00:59:34.397
and this is what was misleading
about the pronouncements

00:59:34.397 --> 00:59:36.198
where they keep saying
over and over again,

00:59:36.198 --> 00:59:38.067
the government's saying
that there's no difference

00:59:38.067 --> 00:59:40.569
in the toxicity of the oil
and oil plus dispersants.

00:59:40.569 --> 00:59:42.872
What that glosses over is that,

00:59:42.872 --> 00:59:44.473
with the oil plus dispersants,

00:59:44.473 --> 00:59:46.942
you have increased the bioavailability,

00:59:46.942 --> 00:59:49.678
which increases the toxic effect.

00:59:49.678 --> 00:59:52.314
It doesn't necessarily
increase the toxicity

00:59:52.314 --> 00:59:56.085
of the molecules, but it
increases the toxic effect

00:59:56.085 --> 00:59:57.953
because it increases bioavailability.

00:59:59.555 --> 01:00:01.557
- Our workers, our fishermen went out

01:00:01.557 --> 01:00:03.692
and worked on the oil spill response,

01:00:03.692 --> 01:00:05.628
and they were told, "Oh,
the oil is nontoxic.

01:00:05.628 --> 01:00:07.897
"It's gonna be as safe as pancake syrup."

01:00:07.897 --> 01:00:10.432
And people got sick.

01:00:10.432 --> 01:00:15.437
People were coughing,
headaches, dizziness, nausea.

01:00:16.472 --> 01:00:18.407
It's like a TB ward out there.

01:00:18.407 --> 01:00:21.143
I mean, everybody's hacking
and choking and coughing.

01:00:21.143 --> 01:00:22.311
What is going on?

01:00:22.311 --> 01:00:23.546
And I say, "Well, I don't know,

01:00:23.546 --> 01:00:26.615
"but surely the medical doctors
will take care of this."

01:00:26.615 --> 01:00:28.684
And again, this is another example of,

01:00:28.684 --> 01:00:30.886
we trusted the medical community.

01:00:30.886 --> 01:00:33.989
We trusted the federal and state laws

01:00:33.989 --> 01:00:36.325
that are put in place to protect workers.

01:00:36.325 --> 01:00:38.627
And it turns out there
was loopholes driven

01:00:38.627 --> 01:00:41.230
into all those laws by the oil companies

01:00:41.230 --> 01:00:43.098
and by the petrochemical industry

01:00:43.098 --> 01:00:47.369
so that those laws all missed
that the workers were sick

01:00:47.369 --> 01:00:49.471
because of oil from the Exxon Valdez

01:00:49.471 --> 01:00:51.674
and the chemical
dispersants that were used.

01:00:51.674 --> 01:00:53.475
So no, nobody took care of them either,

01:00:53.475 --> 01:00:56.712
and I still am dealing
now with cleanup workers

01:00:56.712 --> 01:00:59.381
from the Exxon Valdez 21 years later,

01:00:59.381 --> 01:01:03.519
who are saying, "I thought I
had the Valdez crud in 1989.

01:01:03.519 --> 01:01:04.720
"That's what Exxon told us,

01:01:04.720 --> 01:01:06.488
"just a little cold, a little flu.

01:01:06.488 --> 01:01:08.424
"I didn't think I'd have it for 14 years,

01:01:08.424 --> 01:01:10.859
"18 years, 21 years.

01:01:10.859 --> 01:01:12.194
"What is wrong with me?"

01:01:13.595 --> 01:01:15.364
- [Narrator] Hearing story after story

01:01:15.364 --> 01:01:19.001
of BP's immoral,
profit-motivated operations

01:01:19.001 --> 01:01:23.138
and the accompanying negligence
that caused the explosion,

01:01:23.138 --> 01:01:24.873
which claimed 11 lives,

01:01:26.141 --> 01:01:29.511
the human effects of this spill
were becoming more apparent

01:01:29.511 --> 01:01:30.579
on a visceral level.

01:01:33.148 --> 01:01:33.982
- Whenever I heard

01:01:33.982 --> 01:01:37.352
that they were cleaning
the oil spill in the Gulf,

01:01:37.352 --> 01:01:41.356
I realized that it was
deja vu all over again.

01:01:41.356 --> 01:01:44.393
It was me and my crew out there cleaning

01:01:44.393 --> 01:01:47.496
and becoming sick, not
being able to breathe,

01:01:47.496 --> 01:01:50.132
and suffering with the flu-like symptoms,

01:01:50.132 --> 01:01:51.467
with stomach disorder.

01:01:52.668 --> 01:01:55.737
It would become like bronchitis,

01:01:55.737 --> 01:01:58.474
and when we went to the
doctor, they only said,

01:01:58.474 --> 01:02:02.578
"It's the flu, and it's going
around, and everyone has it."

01:02:02.578 --> 01:02:03.779
- More and more stories

01:02:03.779 --> 01:02:05.881
about sick fishermen are
beginning to surface.

01:02:05.881 --> 01:02:08.750
They're out in the Gulf, many
of them all day every day,

01:02:08.750 --> 01:02:10.319
working to clean up the spill.

01:02:10.319 --> 01:02:12.788
Many blame the chemicals BP is using.

01:02:12.788 --> 01:02:14.223
One fisherman told me tonight,

01:02:14.223 --> 01:02:17.025
he felt like he was going
to die over the weekend.

01:02:19.228 --> 01:02:22.397
- Like a (scoffs) sniffing
gasoline or something.

01:02:24.399 --> 01:02:26.668
And I still, with my ears
are popping right now.

01:02:26.668 --> 01:02:27.970
I'm still coughing up stuff.

01:02:27.970 --> 01:02:31.139
I feel real weak, little tingling.

01:02:31.139 --> 01:02:33.008
- [Reporter] Marine
toxicologist Riki Ott says,

01:02:33.008 --> 01:02:36.578
the chemicals used by BP can
wreak havoc on a person's body

01:02:36.578 --> 01:02:38.080
and even lead to death.

01:02:38.080 --> 01:02:40.415
- With the volatile organic carbons,

01:02:40.415 --> 01:02:42.351
they act like a narcotic on the brain,

01:02:42.351 --> 01:02:44.152
and at high concentrations,

01:02:44.152 --> 01:02:46.088
what we learned at Exxon Valdez

01:02:46.088 --> 01:02:48.624
from carcasses of harbor
seals and sea otters,

01:02:48.624 --> 01:02:52.494
it actually literally fried
the brain, brain lesions.

01:02:52.494 --> 01:02:53.962
- [Reporter] Congressman Charlie Menancon

01:02:53.962 --> 01:02:54.796
wants something done.

01:02:54.796 --> 01:02:56.898
He sent a letter to the
Obama administration,

01:02:56.898 --> 01:02:59.735
calling for temporary
healthcare clinics to be set up.

01:02:59.735 --> 01:03:00.569
- [Congressman] Close by,

01:03:00.569 --> 01:03:03.038
so that there can be immediately attention

01:03:03.038 --> 01:03:05.841
to any people that feel
that they have problems,

01:03:05.841 --> 01:03:09.144
adverse problems that are
caused by the inhalation

01:03:09.144 --> 01:03:13.115
or exposure to the oil.

01:03:13.115 --> 01:03:16.218
- According to testimony
we heard in Louisiana,

01:03:16.218 --> 01:03:18.854
still BP failed to provide respirators

01:03:18.854 --> 01:03:21.423
to the workers exposed to the crude oil,

01:03:21.423 --> 01:03:23.859
and the workers
experienced health impacts.

01:03:23.859 --> 01:03:27.095
The workers were afraid to
speak up due to the potential

01:03:27.095 --> 01:03:28.196
to lose their jobs.

01:03:28.196 --> 01:03:30.999
Those fishermen who
attempted to wear respirators

01:03:30.999 --> 01:03:34.569
while working were
threatened to be fired by BP

01:03:34.569 --> 01:03:37.739
due to the workers using the respirators.

01:03:37.739 --> 01:03:39.174
Do you know anything about that?

01:03:39.174 --> 01:03:40.642
- I'm not aware of that.

01:03:40.642 --> 01:03:42.978
- [Reporter] Now the federal
Environmental Protection Agency

01:03:42.978 --> 01:03:45.013
has been tracking what's in the air

01:03:45.013 --> 01:03:48.517
by using a mobile truck and
seven monitoring stations,

01:03:48.517 --> 01:03:52.120
located mainly in St. Bernard
and Plaquemines Parishes.

01:03:52.120 --> 01:03:54.656
They are looking for evidence
of several compounds,

01:03:54.656 --> 01:03:58.460
including hydrogen sulfide and
volatile organic compounds,

01:03:58.460 --> 01:04:03.198
or VOCs, like Benzyne, which
we commonly know as swamp gas.

01:04:03.198 --> 01:04:06.801
A normal level ranges from
five to 10 parts per billion.

01:04:06.801 --> 01:04:09.104
At the Venice station on May 2nd,

01:04:09.104 --> 01:04:11.840
the levels recorded were
30 parts per billion.

01:04:11.840 --> 01:04:14.843
The next day, the reading
was nearly 40 times that,

01:04:14.843 --> 01:04:17.212
at nearly 1200 parts per billion,

01:04:17.212 --> 01:04:20.282
and the day after that,
it dropped down to 46

01:04:20.282 --> 01:04:23.318
before rising the next
day to more than 1000.

01:04:24.219 --> 01:04:26.054
- The air quality's not good.

01:04:26.054 --> 01:04:27.355
We've been having trouble, you know.

01:04:27.355 --> 01:04:31.059
I've been seeing people sick,
their eyes crying a lot,

01:04:31.059 --> 01:04:35.430
nasal passages stopped up, sore throats.

01:04:37.599 --> 01:04:38.967
I don't think it's safe.

01:04:38.967 --> 01:04:41.236
My wife just refused to leave.

01:04:41.236 --> 01:04:43.204
- My daughter got sick.
- How old is your daughter?

01:04:43.204 --> 01:04:45.440
- It was my daughter, she's 14.

01:04:46.474 --> 01:04:47.609
We took her to the emergency room.

01:04:47.609 --> 01:04:49.377
She was having a headache
real bad and all the time,

01:04:49.377 --> 01:04:51.880
so we took her to the emergency
room and they did tests,

01:04:51.880 --> 01:04:54.149
and they couldn't find
nothing wrong with her.

01:04:54.149 --> 01:04:55.550
And the doctor said, "Follow
up with another doctor"

01:04:55.550 --> 01:04:56.518
"if she still had 'em,"
so she still had 'em.

01:04:56.518 --> 01:04:58.186
So we took her to another doctor,

01:04:58.186 --> 01:05:00.188
and they drew blood and they...

01:05:00.188 --> 01:05:02.757
and they found chemicals
in her urine and all,

01:05:03.725 --> 01:05:04.793
different kind of chemicals.

01:05:04.793 --> 01:05:07.328
- [Reporter] The president
also came here as a salesman,

01:05:07.328 --> 01:05:10.098
to convince vacationers to come on down.

01:05:10.098 --> 01:05:14.202
- Beaches all along the
Gulf Coast are clean.

01:05:14.202 --> 01:05:17.305
They are safe, and they
are open for business.

01:05:17.305 --> 01:05:19.741
That's one of the reasons
Michelle, Sasha, and I are here.

01:05:19.741 --> 01:05:22.744
- News 5 is doing some testing of our own.

01:05:22.744 --> 01:05:25.780
News 5 investigation
took us from Gulf Shores

01:05:25.780 --> 01:05:30.785
to Orange Beach to Katrina Key
to the Dauphin Island Marina.

01:05:30.785 --> 01:05:33.588
We collected samples of water, sand,

01:05:33.588 --> 01:05:35.723
and when we saw kids digging:

01:05:35.723 --> 01:05:37.458
Do you mind if we get some water

01:05:37.458 --> 01:05:39.527
out of your sand hole here?

01:05:39.527 --> 01:05:41.362
We got a sample of that, too.

01:05:41.362 --> 01:05:46.067
Bob Newman is a chemist with
nearly 30 years of experience.

01:05:46.067 --> 01:05:49.470
He tested our water and
sand for oil and petroleum.

01:05:49.470 --> 01:05:53.074
Gulf Shores beach water, right
where people were swimming,

01:05:53.074 --> 01:05:56.110
showed 66 parts per million.

01:05:56.110 --> 01:05:58.780
And the sand where beach-goers are walking

01:05:58.780 --> 01:06:02.483
has 211 parts per million.

01:06:02.483 --> 01:06:05.820
The sixth sample was a spot
in Orange Beach where, again,

01:06:05.820 --> 01:06:07.922
we found kids playing,

01:06:07.922 --> 01:06:11.793
and we found our highest
content of oil and petroleum,

01:06:11.793 --> 01:06:15.964
221 parts per million.

01:06:15.964 --> 01:06:18.433
But the tests didn't stop there.

01:06:18.433 --> 01:06:20.969
The sample we haven't
yet told you about is

01:06:20.969 --> 01:06:23.504
the one collected from
Dauphin Island Marina.

01:06:23.504 --> 01:06:28.009
The sample exploded right in his lab.

01:06:28.009 --> 01:06:29.677
- Almost instantaneous,

01:06:29.677 --> 01:06:31.412
actually maybe one second.

01:06:33.648 --> 01:06:34.482
It's just weird.

01:06:34.482 --> 01:06:38.086
- [Reporter] The result
surprised even our chemist.

01:06:38.086 --> 01:06:41.923
- We think that it most likely
happened due to the presence

01:06:41.923 --> 01:06:46.394
of either ethanol or methane gas

01:06:46.394 --> 01:06:49.330
or the presence of the
dispersant that corrects it.

01:06:49.330 --> 01:06:52.867
- Across the Gulf, there
were stories of sick people,

01:06:52.867 --> 01:06:57.438
I mean, emergency room,
puking brown, peeing brown.

01:06:57.438 --> 01:06:59.207
This is internal hemorrhaging.

01:06:59.207 --> 01:07:00.742
This is what the EPA whistleblower

01:07:00.742 --> 01:07:02.243
Hugh Kaufman was talking about.

01:07:02.243 --> 01:07:04.712
This is what the material
safety datasheets talk about,

01:07:04.712 --> 01:07:06.114
internal hemorrhaging.

01:07:06.114 --> 01:07:09.984
- I think that the use of
dispersants is criminal.

01:07:09.984 --> 01:07:14.689
I mean, essentially, by
allowing BP to use dispersants

01:07:14.689 --> 01:07:17.658
rather than forcing them to
come and skim and remove the oil

01:07:17.658 --> 01:07:20.628
from the top of the Gulf surface,

01:07:20.628 --> 01:07:24.031
we have legally allowed
them to use the Gulf,

01:07:24.031 --> 01:07:26.501
our gulf, as their disposal unit for oil.

01:07:27.568 --> 01:07:29.871
Dispersants don't make the oil go away.

01:07:29.871 --> 01:07:31.239
They simply make it smaller,

01:07:31.239 --> 01:07:33.074
they make it dissolve into the water,

01:07:33.074 --> 01:07:36.544
they make it break down
to where, it's true,

01:07:36.544 --> 01:07:38.613
to where oil-eating
microbes can break it down

01:07:38.613 --> 01:07:40.748
at a faster rate, absolutely true.

01:07:40.748 --> 01:07:41.816
But what it also means is,

01:07:41.816 --> 01:07:44.418
the other billions of
organisms in the Gulf

01:07:44.418 --> 01:07:48.089
that aren't oil-eating
microbes can also absorb it

01:07:48.089 --> 01:07:50.925
and bioaccumulate it
at a much faster rate.

01:07:50.925 --> 01:07:52.426
There are many studies after Valdez,

01:07:52.426 --> 01:07:57.131
for a decade after Exxon
Valdez and the overall message

01:07:57.131 --> 01:07:59.901
from all of these peer-reviewed
scientific studies was,

01:07:59.901 --> 01:08:02.270
dispersants, including Corexit,

01:08:03.404 --> 01:08:06.340
increased the bioaccumulation of the PAHs,

01:08:06.340 --> 01:08:08.075
the carcinogenic component of oil

01:08:08.075 --> 01:08:12.146
by anywhere from 12 to 50,000 times.

01:08:12.146 --> 01:08:16.183
So our laws are set up to
where BP was allowed to dispose

01:08:16.183 --> 01:08:20.054
of their oil into our gulf's
food web, essentially.

01:08:21.489 --> 01:08:22.423
Big mistake.

01:08:22.423 --> 01:08:24.492
- News report that they had,

01:08:24.492 --> 01:08:29.463
it said that the snapper
and mullet were safe to eat.

01:08:30.331 --> 01:08:32.800
Well, you can catch snapper in this bay.

01:08:32.800 --> 01:08:34.468
Snapper and mullet were safe to eat,

01:08:34.468 --> 01:08:37.638
but shrimp and crabs, stay away from 'em.

01:08:37.638 --> 01:08:39.807
They're all habitat in the same bay.

01:08:40.708 --> 01:08:42.176
Nowhere else, they're in this bay.

01:08:42.176 --> 01:08:44.345
I mean, if you wanna say,

01:08:44.345 --> 01:08:47.181
"Well, how can snapper and mullet be safe

01:08:47.181 --> 01:08:50.684
"and shrimp and crab side-by-side,"

01:08:50.684 --> 01:08:54.021
I mean, literally,
side-by-side, they're not safe.

01:08:54.021 --> 01:08:55.423
Something don't make
sense with that picture.

01:08:55.423 --> 01:08:58.426
That picture doesn't sell with me at all.

01:09:00.161 --> 01:09:02.229
If that was meant to be said,

01:09:02.229 --> 01:09:04.865
and they said it that way, they're wrong.

01:09:04.865 --> 01:09:06.567
And I still wouldn't sell anything

01:09:06.567 --> 01:09:10.738
until all of these species are safe.

01:09:10.738 --> 01:09:13.207
- The dispersed oil is
gonna affect all of that.

01:09:13.207 --> 01:09:16.043
It's gonna affect the
eggs, the larvae, the food,

01:09:16.043 --> 01:09:18.712
everything in the plankton
is gonna be impacted.

01:09:18.712 --> 01:09:23.184
And so that area that's
been affected is...

01:09:24.418 --> 01:09:27.221
You can't, probably shouldn't
go as far as saying sterilized

01:09:27.221 --> 01:09:29.390
but, I mean, the impact is huge.

01:09:29.390 --> 01:09:30.624
- My livelihood, the money

01:09:30.624 --> 01:09:33.861
that I live off of comes
from selling fish and stuff.

01:09:33.861 --> 01:09:35.129
Yeah, I want it to be open,

01:09:35.129 --> 01:09:38.132
but would I personally
eat anything that I catch?

01:09:38.132 --> 01:09:40.834
I certainly have my doubts.

01:09:40.834 --> 01:09:42.369
I would be a little bit apprehensive.

01:09:42.369 --> 01:09:45.472
- We expect to see a substantial reduction

01:09:45.472 --> 01:09:48.409
in the overall amount of dispersant used,

01:09:48.409 --> 01:09:51.478
primarily because they
shouldn't need to be spraying it

01:09:51.478 --> 01:09:53.714
if they're able to disperse it earlier.

01:09:53.714 --> 01:09:56.750
If we start to see dispersing
chemicals in that water

01:09:56.750 --> 01:09:58.652
or anywhere in the water column,

01:09:59.954 --> 01:10:01.221
we're gonna recommend strongly

01:10:01.221 --> 01:10:03.190
to the on-scene coordinator that we stop.

01:10:03.190 --> 01:10:04.191
- It was everywhere.

01:10:05.225 --> 01:10:07.261
Obviously, certain days
would be worse than others,

01:10:07.261 --> 01:10:10.364
depending on how strong
of a south wind we had

01:10:10.364 --> 01:10:11.932
or what the current was doing.

01:10:14.335 --> 01:10:16.103
As I've said before, it was weird how,

01:10:16.103 --> 01:10:19.273
being a fisherman and knowing
the Gulf like I feel I know it

01:10:19.273 --> 01:10:22.776
knowing what direction and
how fast the current's moving

01:10:22.776 --> 01:10:24.478
and what the wind's doing, I mean,

01:10:24.478 --> 01:10:27.114
there was absolutely no rhyme or reason

01:10:27.114 --> 01:10:30.351
to being in the stuff for
two weeks straight everywhere

01:10:30.351 --> 01:10:33.187
and knowing that there was a
bunch of it to the east of us

01:10:33.187 --> 01:10:34.421
when we've got a current out of the east,

01:10:34.421 --> 01:10:36.824
and it should be just right
over here the next day

01:10:36.824 --> 01:10:39.426
and it's gone, just little white bubbles.

01:10:41.895 --> 01:10:44.364
We heard rumors, and I can't confirm it,

01:10:44.364 --> 01:10:46.834
but I mean, there's not doubt
they were sinking the stuff.

01:10:46.834 --> 01:10:49.203
They were spraying it
with airplanes at night.

01:10:49.203 --> 01:10:51.038
I've got some friends, I'm
not gonna mention their names,

01:10:51.038 --> 01:10:53.574
but they worked offshore, stayed offshore

01:10:53.574 --> 01:10:55.576
for several days at a time
and they would tell 'em,

01:10:55.576 --> 01:10:57.444
"Between 12 and four, stay in your cabins

01:10:57.444 --> 01:11:00.848
"because we're gonna bomb
it, we're gonna spray it."

01:11:00.848 --> 01:11:02.916
From my understanding, that was not legal.

01:11:02.916 --> 01:11:04.284
That was not authorized by the government

01:11:04.284 --> 01:11:06.253
for them to do that, they did it anyway.

01:11:06.253 --> 01:11:08.222
Out of sight, out of mind
has been their policy,

01:11:08.222 --> 01:11:10.324
and they lived up to that part of it.

01:11:10.324 --> 01:11:12.159
- The BP lied from day one,

01:11:12.159 --> 01:11:13.494
and they're still lying to people,

01:11:13.494 --> 01:11:15.863
so what's the difference?

01:11:15.863 --> 01:11:18.398
We ask them to quit
spraying that disperser

01:11:18.398 --> 01:11:21.068
'cause that's a killer, but
they wanna keep on hiding it.

01:11:21.068 --> 01:11:23.704
They keep on spraying,
they wanna hide that oil

01:11:23.704 --> 01:11:26.240
under the water where nobody can see it,

01:11:26.240 --> 01:11:27.207
which is wrong.

01:11:27.207 --> 01:11:29.543
Let the son of a bitch come
to the top of the water.

01:11:29.543 --> 01:11:30.444
Let them skim it.

01:11:40.287 --> 01:11:42.456
- I've been on record
for a long time being

01:11:42.456 --> 01:11:43.991
against use of that dispersant.

01:11:43.991 --> 01:11:45.325
I'm not against use of a dispersant.

01:11:45.325 --> 01:11:47.527
There's a lot of organic dispersants,

01:11:47.527 --> 01:11:49.096
and that's what I was
talking about earlier.

01:11:49.096 --> 01:11:51.765
People can't get their products
to BP to be considered.

01:11:51.765 --> 01:11:53.700
I've seen a lot a organic dispersants

01:11:53.700 --> 01:11:55.235
that, one, coulda been used.

01:11:55.235 --> 01:11:57.037
I have a problem with
the dispersants they used

01:11:57.037 --> 01:11:58.772
'cause it's Corexit.

01:11:58.772 --> 01:12:02.142
There's corporate relationships between BP

01:12:02.142 --> 01:12:05.312
and the company that manufactures Corexit.

01:12:05.312 --> 01:12:06.680
It's banned in the United Kingdom.

01:12:06.680 --> 01:12:08.215
If it's not good enough
for the United Kingdom,

01:12:08.215 --> 01:12:09.049
it shouldn't be good enough

01:12:09.049 --> 01:12:10.417
for the Gulf of Mexico, number one.

01:12:10.417 --> 01:12:14.421
Number two, I think this
dispersant may be...

01:12:14.421 --> 01:12:16.556
The solution may be
worse than the problem.

01:12:17.557 --> 01:12:21.228
And three, the dispersant
has actually complicated

01:12:21.228 --> 01:12:23.630
the skimming of this oil because
it's forced it underneath

01:12:23.630 --> 01:12:26.533
and it's also been like
trying to track a ghost.

01:12:28.468 --> 01:12:30.804
Again, I think the Federal
Government failed us there

01:12:30.804 --> 01:12:33.407
because, again, just like we talked about,

01:12:33.407 --> 01:12:36.676
for MMS now the department
of ocean and energy,

01:12:36.676 --> 01:12:40.647
the EPA has been basically
a lapdog and not a watchdog

01:12:40.647 --> 01:12:43.150
because they've requested
and they've said on record

01:12:43.150 --> 01:12:44.918
they have told BP not to
use it, but guess what.

01:12:44.918 --> 01:12:47.020
They continue to use it despite that.

01:12:47.020 --> 01:12:48.722
- EPA told 'em, stop using it

01:12:48.722 --> 01:12:51.158
because it's toxic to human beings.

01:12:51.158 --> 01:12:52.392
BP said no.
- Right.

01:12:52.392 --> 01:12:54.127
- Now we've learned there's
not anything else out there

01:12:54.127 --> 01:12:56.029
in the kind of quantity they need.

01:12:56.029 --> 01:12:58.365
So they're putting something
toxic to human beings

01:12:58.365 --> 01:13:01.635
and harmful to sea life in the sea?

01:13:03.270 --> 01:13:04.104
- You know, what's interesting,

01:13:04.104 --> 01:13:05.305
Shepherd.
- Not to me!

01:13:06.373 --> 01:13:09.109
- An Exxon report some years ago showed

01:13:10.010 --> 01:13:12.879
the Corexit is actually
four times more toxic

01:13:12.879 --> 01:13:14.981
than the crude oil itself.
- Would you say that again?

01:13:14.981 --> 01:13:17.083
- In this case, these
dispersants were used

01:13:17.083 --> 01:13:21.588
in massive quantities, almost
two million gallons so far,

01:13:21.588 --> 01:13:26.226
to hide the magnitude of
the spill and save BP money.

01:13:26.226 --> 01:13:30.030
And the government, both
EPA, NOAA, et cetera,

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:34.501
have been sock puppets
for BP in this coverup.

01:13:34.501 --> 01:13:38.738
Now by hiding the amount of spill,

01:13:38.738 --> 01:13:41.641
BP is saving hundreds of millions,

01:13:41.641 --> 01:13:44.277
if not billions of dollars in fines.

01:13:44.277 --> 01:13:48.481
And so from day one, there was
tremendous economic incentive

01:13:48.481 --> 01:13:53.486
to use these dispersants
to hide the magnitude

01:13:53.853 --> 01:13:57.791
of the gusher that's been going
on for almost three months.

01:13:57.791 --> 01:14:00.627
One thing that I did
wanna mention to you, Amy,

01:14:00.627 --> 01:14:03.129
that's occurred in most investigations,

01:14:03.129 --> 01:14:06.299
back even in the Watergate
days, people said,

01:14:06.299 --> 01:14:09.002
"Follow the money," and that's correct.

01:14:09.002 --> 01:14:11.338
In this case, you've
gotta follow the money.

01:14:11.338 --> 01:14:15.709
Who saves money by using
these toxic dispersants?

01:14:15.709 --> 01:14:16.910
Well, it's BP.

01:14:16.910 --> 01:14:18.378
but then the next question,

01:14:18.378 --> 01:14:20.814
I've only seen one
article that describes it,

01:14:20.814 --> 01:14:22.515
who owns BP?

01:14:22.515 --> 01:14:26.419
(intrepid pensive piano music)

01:14:51.377 --> 01:14:53.713
When you look and see who owns BP,

01:14:53.713 --> 01:14:56.316
you find that the majority ownership,

01:14:56.316 --> 01:14:59.452
a billion shares is a
company called BlackRock.

01:14:59.452 --> 01:15:01.421
Vanity Fair just did recently an article

01:15:01.421 --> 01:15:04.958
about Mr. Fink and his
connections with Mr. Geithner,

01:15:04.958 --> 01:15:08.194
Mr. Summers and others
in the administration.

01:15:08.194 --> 01:15:09.929
So I think what's needed,

01:15:09.929 --> 01:15:12.131
we now know that there's a coverup.

01:15:12.131 --> 01:15:14.100
- [Narrator] It was becoming obvious

01:15:14.100 --> 01:15:16.502
that Americans could
no longer blindly trust

01:15:16.502 --> 01:15:18.905
their watchdog agencies.

01:15:20.807 --> 01:15:22.442
- Today the Federal
Government is releasing

01:15:22.442 --> 01:15:25.278
a new scientific analysis
that addresses the question:

01:15:25.278 --> 01:15:27.647
Where did the oil go?

01:15:27.647 --> 01:15:30.850
The key conclusions of the report is

01:15:30.850 --> 01:15:34.320
that the vast majority of
the oil has either evaporated

01:15:34.320 --> 01:15:35.955
or been burned, skimmed,

01:15:35.955 --> 01:15:39.359
and recovered from the
wellhead, or dispersed.

01:15:39.359 --> 01:15:42.128
And much of the dispersed
oil is in the process

01:15:42.128 --> 01:15:44.697
of relatively rapid degradation.

01:15:44.697 --> 01:15:49.569
A significant amount of
this is a direct result

01:15:49.569 --> 01:15:52.739
of the very robust
federal response efforts.

01:15:52.739 --> 01:15:55.641
- New evidence has badly shaken

01:15:55.641 --> 01:15:58.077
the Obama administration's rosy narrative

01:15:58.077 --> 01:16:00.646
about the alleged disappearance
of most of the oil

01:16:00.646 --> 01:16:02.348
that gushed into the Gulf of Mexico

01:16:02.348 --> 01:16:04.350
from BP's blown out well.

01:16:04.350 --> 01:16:05.351
Early this month,

01:16:05.351 --> 01:16:07.520
the report by government
scientists declared

01:16:07.520 --> 01:16:09.622
3/4 of the oil had vanished,

01:16:09.622 --> 01:16:11.791
either collected or dispersed.

01:16:11.791 --> 01:16:13.826
But numerous reports contradict

01:16:13.826 --> 01:16:17.130
the administration's sanguine
picture of the cleanup effort.

01:16:17.130 --> 01:16:19.632
Researchers at the
University of Georgia said

01:16:19.632 --> 01:16:22.769
about 3/4 of the oil is still
lurking below the surface

01:16:22.769 --> 01:16:25.872
of the Gulf and may pose
a threat to the ecosystem.

01:16:25.872 --> 01:16:28.541
Separately, a study released last week

01:16:28.541 --> 01:16:31.277
by the University of South
Florida scientists found oil

01:16:31.277 --> 01:16:33.279
and sediments of an underwater canyon

01:16:33.279 --> 01:16:36.048
and evidence that the oil has become toxic

01:16:36.048 --> 01:16:38.217
to critical marine organisms.

01:16:38.217 --> 01:16:41.287
On Thursday, a team of researchers
confirmed the existence

01:16:41.287 --> 01:16:44.757
of a vast underwater oil
plume stretching 21 miles

01:16:44.757 --> 01:16:46.959
from BP's blown out well.

01:16:46.959 --> 01:16:50.029
Christopher Reddy of Woods
Hole Oceanographic institution

01:16:50.029 --> 01:16:52.932
said the amount of oil
in the plume is unknown.

01:16:54.634 --> 01:16:59.205
- [Narrator] Now it was
clear, how and why BP,

01:16:59.205 --> 01:17:01.541
with the help of the Federal Government,

01:17:01.541 --> 01:17:03.743
willfully caused this disaster

01:17:03.743 --> 01:17:06.646
and continually tried
to sweep this toxic soup

01:17:06.646 --> 01:17:07.480
under the rug.

01:17:08.481 --> 01:17:10.816
- I don't care if you're
a Democrat, Republican,

01:17:10.816 --> 01:17:12.885
Independent, Green Party.

01:17:12.885 --> 01:17:15.254
Look, let's face it.

01:17:16.322 --> 01:17:20.359
They dropped the ball in
not protesting these toxins

01:17:20.359 --> 01:17:24.163
or these dispersants
being spread in our water.

01:17:24.163 --> 01:17:25.631
We don't know the longterm effect.

01:17:25.631 --> 01:17:27.800
- [Narrator] If that wasn't bad enough,

01:17:27.800 --> 01:17:32.738
the desperate out-of-work Gulf
Cost residents were forced

01:17:32.738 --> 01:17:36.375
to do the hazardous sweeping
without proper safety geer.

01:17:38.778 --> 01:17:43.783
The final insult was the cleanup
workers gross underpayment,

01:17:43.783 --> 01:17:47.086
which leads us to the last
subject: compensation.

01:17:47.086 --> 01:17:50.156
(typewriter keys clicking)

01:17:50.156 --> 01:17:52.925
- The only way you intend
to pay those claims is

01:17:52.925 --> 01:17:54.927
if it comes through this fund.

01:17:54.927 --> 01:17:55.795
Is that your testimony today?

01:17:55.795 --> 01:17:59.432
- $20 billion is a very large
sum of money to pay claims.

01:17:59.432 --> 01:18:00.266
- I'm sorry?

01:18:00.266 --> 01:18:03.769
- $20 billion is a very
large fund to pay claims.

01:18:03.769 --> 01:18:06.605
- It is a large fund, and is it your view

01:18:06.605 --> 01:18:09.809
that part of what that
fund will be used for is

01:18:09.809 --> 01:18:13.712
to pay people for their
longterm healthcare costs

01:18:13.712 --> 01:18:15.481
incurred as a result of this spill.

01:18:15.481 --> 01:18:19.185
- That will be a decision for
the independent adjudicator.

01:18:19.185 --> 01:18:20.986
- Well, if they asked
you what you thought,

01:18:20.986 --> 01:18:21.854
would you say, yes,

01:18:21.854 --> 01:18:24.123
the healthcare costs should
be paid for from this.

01:18:24.123 --> 01:18:26.091
- I think that's something that
I will leave him to decide.

01:18:26.091 --> 01:18:29.228
That's why we've appointed
an independent adjudicator.

01:18:29.228 --> 01:18:30.162
- So...

01:18:30.162 --> 01:18:35.000
- He's the guy that settled
all of the Agent Orange suits

01:18:36.535 --> 01:18:37.837
from the Vietnam War.

01:18:39.405 --> 01:18:43.542
He's the fella that was the
trust fund administrator

01:18:43.542 --> 01:18:47.546
for 9/11 after the collapse
of the World Trade Center,

01:18:48.547 --> 01:18:51.183
and you can imagine how
complicated that was,

01:18:52.318 --> 01:18:54.220
especially with all of the deaths

01:18:54.220 --> 01:18:56.155
that occurred from that tragedy.

01:18:57.389 --> 01:19:02.194
He is the fella that was
called upon to Virginia

01:19:02.194 --> 01:19:07.199
in the Virginia Tech Massacre
to solve those problems.

01:19:07.299 --> 01:19:12.304
Here we have the world's expert to step in

01:19:12.771 --> 01:19:15.908
and smooth out this process and to see

01:19:15.908 --> 01:19:19.144
that just and due compensation is given

01:19:19.144 --> 01:19:24.149
to people who have suffered as
so many of us have suffered.

01:19:24.516 --> 01:19:25.718
Ken Feinberg.

01:19:25.718 --> 01:19:28.721
(audience applauds)

01:19:31.090 --> 01:19:34.326
- I wanna thank Senator Nelson.

01:19:34.326 --> 01:19:36.862
I'm here because Senator
Nelson said, "Be here,"

01:19:36.862 --> 01:19:40.933
and when Senator Nelson
asks you to do something,

01:19:40.933 --> 01:19:42.001
you do.

01:19:42.001 --> 01:19:43.836
- What's going to happen to our fish,

01:19:43.836 --> 01:19:45.971
our crabs, our shrimp, our oysters?

01:19:45.971 --> 01:19:48.007
People are not buying our seafood now

01:19:48.007 --> 01:19:49.508
because of these toxins.

01:19:49.508 --> 01:19:52.578
How can British Petroleum
make us whole on that issue?

01:19:52.578 --> 01:19:54.847
- You seem to be saying

01:19:55.714 --> 01:19:57.683
that our oysters,

01:19:57.683 --> 01:20:00.085
that there's gonna be longterm,

01:20:00.085 --> 01:20:03.322
potential longterm devastation

01:20:03.322 --> 01:20:06.158
to the ability to catch quota.

01:20:07.092 --> 01:20:08.861
I don't know if that's true.

01:20:08.861 --> 01:20:10.295
Do you know if that's true?

01:20:10.295 --> 01:20:13.031
There may be no longterm impact.

01:20:13.031 --> 01:20:15.467
Senator Nelson mentions my credibility,

01:20:16.568 --> 01:20:18.604
doing these other projects.

01:20:18.604 --> 01:20:22.608
My credibility will last
a very, very short time

01:20:23.642 --> 01:20:26.512
if the money doesn't get delivered here

01:20:26.512 --> 01:20:31.517
to eligible claimants
beginning when I take over

01:20:32.518 --> 01:20:36.522
with the Gulf Coast claims
facility on August 23rd,

01:20:36.522 --> 01:20:38.757
less than two weeks away.

01:20:38.757 --> 01:20:41.326
- [Reporter] BP has
pounded home this message:

01:20:41.326 --> 01:20:43.195
- [Man's Voice] BP has
got to make things right.

01:20:43.195 --> 01:20:46.331
- And they're spending
$100 million on a PR blitz

01:20:46.331 --> 01:20:49.401
to tell the rest of the country
they're making us whole.

01:20:49.401 --> 01:20:51.737
Guys, it's not true, it's not true.

01:20:51.737 --> 01:20:55.540
- [Reporter) And Mayor Kennon
Of Orange Beach, Alabama,

01:20:55.540 --> 01:20:58.010
devastated when tourists stayed away,

01:20:58.010 --> 01:21:00.746
says the new independent
claims administrator

01:21:00.746 --> 01:21:03.916
Ken Feinberg has not made things better.

01:21:03.916 --> 01:21:06.418
- So we're very upset with
the overall performance

01:21:06.418 --> 01:21:07.352
of Ken Feinberg.

01:21:07.352 --> 01:21:08.420
- [Reporter] In an interview,

01:21:08.420 --> 01:21:10.689
Feinberg acknowledges problems.

01:21:10.689 --> 01:21:13.258
- We haven't processed all of the claims

01:21:13.258 --> 01:21:15.427
as quickly as we should've.

01:21:15.427 --> 01:21:17.529
I oversold this program at the beginning,

01:21:17.529 --> 01:21:18.797
and I'm paying the price for it.

01:21:18.797 --> 01:21:20.832
I do not work for BP.

01:21:20.832 --> 01:21:22.768
- I'm still sitting over here.

01:21:22.768 --> 01:21:24.369
They cut me instead of helping me.

01:21:24.369 --> 01:21:27.839
They cut me from 5000 to 2300 a month,

01:21:27.839 --> 01:21:28.774
so you tell me.

01:21:30.909 --> 01:21:34.079
(typewriter keys clicking)

01:21:34.079 --> 01:21:35.247
- What's happened with,

01:21:35.247 --> 01:21:37.516
and it's not just the BP scenario.

01:21:37.516 --> 01:21:40.218
It happens across the board.

01:21:40.218 --> 01:21:41.720
In America, we have this,

01:21:41.720 --> 01:21:44.423
because we value capitalism so much,

01:21:45.957 --> 01:21:48.927
and capitalism really is a great system.

01:21:48.927 --> 01:21:51.496
It's potentially one of the
best systems in the world

01:21:51.496 --> 01:21:54.499
but has become so
remarkably flawed lately.

01:21:54.499 --> 01:21:57.269
Part of the flaw is that the people

01:21:57.269 --> 01:21:58.737
that you talk to along the coast,

01:21:58.737 --> 01:22:00.572
probably if you ask 'em what their sense

01:22:00.572 --> 01:22:02.407
of corporate America is,

01:22:02.407 --> 01:22:06.111
they don't really see them
as criminals sometimes.

01:22:06.111 --> 01:22:09.681
Now if we dress 'em up in an Armani suit

01:22:09.681 --> 01:22:11.850
and we put $500 shoes on 'em,

01:22:11.850 --> 01:22:14.820
and we put Rolex watches on their hands,

01:22:14.820 --> 01:22:16.988
and we put 'em in a silk tie,

01:22:16.988 --> 01:22:20.826
all of a sudden they may
be completely a psychopath.

01:22:22.093 --> 01:22:23.695
In their heart, they're still a criminal.

01:22:23.695 --> 01:22:25.263
We look at 'em differently

01:22:25.263 --> 01:22:29.935
than we do the kid who has
three ounces of pot in a bag

01:22:29.935 --> 01:22:32.671
that we throw into prison for two years.

01:22:32.671 --> 01:22:34.105
Now, a corporation in this setting,

01:22:34.105 --> 01:22:37.075
let's just say what it
is: They killed 11 people.

01:22:37.075 --> 01:22:38.410
- [Interviewer] Right, right.

01:22:38.410 --> 01:22:41.046
- For all practical purposes,
they created mass murder,

01:22:41.046 --> 01:22:44.049
but still you can't find
people understanding

01:22:44.049 --> 01:22:46.952
that they're criminals
because they look different.

01:22:46.952 --> 01:22:48.620
They're from Wall Street.

01:22:48.620 --> 01:22:50.956
They have a British accent.

01:22:50.956 --> 01:22:53.258
They're dressed up in an Armani suit.

01:22:53.258 --> 01:22:55.327
They have nice looking shoes.

01:22:55.327 --> 01:22:57.062
They talk intelligently.

01:22:57.062 --> 01:22:59.097
They have dinner with politicians.

01:22:59.097 --> 01:23:00.632
They know celebrities.

01:23:00.632 --> 01:23:03.068
And our culture doesn't
allow us to understand

01:23:03.068 --> 01:23:04.970
that they're really just criminals.

01:23:06.338 --> 01:23:07.973
- [Narrator] The year before the spill,

01:23:07.973 --> 01:23:11.509
BP sales equaled $239 billion.

01:23:13.945 --> 01:23:18.950
BP's total fines and settlements
were a meager 0.3% of this.

01:23:22.020 --> 01:23:23.688
The oil industry is, by far,

01:23:23.688 --> 01:23:26.591
the most profitable industry in the world

01:23:26.591 --> 01:23:30.562
and includes six of the 10
largest corporations worldwide.

01:23:31.463 --> 01:23:34.399
This industry is now
experiencing a level of power

01:23:34.399 --> 01:23:37.235
comparable only to the
era of Standard Oil.

01:23:39.671 --> 01:23:42.707
We are currently standing
at the crossroads.

01:23:42.707 --> 01:23:45.844
The choices we make in the
next five years will impact

01:23:45.844 --> 01:23:49.080
our future generations like never before.

01:23:49.080 --> 01:23:53.284
Much of the technology and
expertise are readily available.

01:23:53.284 --> 01:23:55.019
It's a matter of choice.

01:23:55.920 --> 01:23:59.090
- When anybody could
convince 13% of idiots

01:23:59.090 --> 01:24:01.059
to go vote for you, you could win.

01:24:02.460 --> 01:24:05.997
The most disappointing part
of the American public is

01:24:05.997 --> 01:24:08.833
that one of the few rights
you got left is to go vote,

01:24:08.833 --> 01:24:12.070
and a lot a people just don't
take the time and don't care,

01:24:12.070 --> 01:24:13.738
and look where we at, you know?

01:24:13.738 --> 01:24:15.773
- [Narrator] We must summon the willpower

01:24:15.773 --> 01:24:19.611
to force government,
corporations, and Wall Street

01:24:19.611 --> 01:24:22.013
to become responsible for their actions

01:24:22.013 --> 01:24:24.215
before we reach the point of no return.

01:24:27.318 --> 01:24:28.353
- We just...

01:24:30.722 --> 01:24:34.125
The future is up for grabs, you might say.

01:24:35.760 --> 01:24:37.362
It really is up for grabs,

01:24:37.362 --> 01:24:40.498
and we gotta do something about it.

01:24:40.498 --> 01:24:42.600
And the only people that are
gonna do something about it,

01:24:42.600 --> 01:24:45.470
you just mentioned, are
the people that care.

01:24:45.470 --> 01:24:48.606
And that's most people,
that's most people.

01:24:48.606 --> 01:24:50.308
Most people care about their country.

01:24:50.308 --> 01:24:51.909
Most people care about this.

01:24:53.678 --> 01:24:56.080
The dissidents and the
radicals get the exposure

01:24:56.080 --> 01:24:58.616
from that press you were
talking about a while ago.

01:25:00.818 --> 01:25:02.553
You have a peace rally in one corner,

01:25:02.553 --> 01:25:03.988
and you have a war rally in the other.

01:25:03.988 --> 01:25:05.123
Who gets the coverage?

01:25:06.257 --> 01:25:08.226
This peace rally's not
gonna get the coverage.

01:25:08.226 --> 01:25:09.060
The war rally's gonna get the coverage.

01:25:10.461 --> 01:25:11.796
It's what people are gonna watch.

01:25:11.796 --> 01:25:14.932
It's what the news, it's
gonna sell the newspapers.

01:25:14.932 --> 01:25:17.368
We've got some changing
to do, young people.

01:25:20.204 --> 01:25:22.173
- I was just talking to
my daughter yesterday.

01:25:22.173 --> 01:25:26.077
I got on a long harangue
about all kinds of things,

01:25:26.077 --> 01:25:28.813
hoping that they did a
better job than we (laughs)

01:25:28.813 --> 01:25:30.048
than we do.

01:25:33.351 --> 01:25:38.356
I'm hoping that our children
learn a better balance

01:25:39.724 --> 01:25:42.393
with the environment, with the economy,

01:25:42.393 --> 01:25:47.064
with the things you need
out of the environment.

01:25:47.064 --> 01:25:50.067
There just needs to be
a better stewardship.

01:25:50.067 --> 01:25:51.969
- Native elders say
that, you know, I mean.

01:25:51.969 --> 01:25:53.271
If you listen to them,

01:25:54.205 --> 01:25:56.107
they talk about the end-time prophecies

01:25:56.107 --> 01:25:57.875
and all this kind of
stuff and they're like,

01:25:57.875 --> 01:25:59.644
"Oh, everybody's saying
it's the end of the world,

01:25:59.644 --> 01:26:00.878
"it's the end of the world,"

01:26:00.878 --> 01:26:02.747
and this native elder says,

01:26:02.747 --> 01:26:04.115
"The world's not gonna end.

01:26:05.283 --> 01:26:10.254
"Humans, humans may end, but
the world will still be here."

01:26:15.126 --> 01:26:17.261
There's an old Chippewa prophecy

01:26:17.261 --> 01:26:19.564
from Sun Bear back in the '20s.

01:26:19.564 --> 01:26:20.631
He said, "There will come a day

01:26:20.631 --> 01:26:23.801
"when the Earth will shake
herself to rid herself

01:26:23.801 --> 01:26:26.037
"of pesky humans like a dog shakes itself

01:26:26.037 --> 01:26:27.705
"to rid itself of fleas."

01:26:29.707 --> 01:26:31.642
You gotta look at the
things that are happening.

01:26:31.642 --> 01:26:33.477
I mean, there's stuff happening around,

01:26:33.477 --> 01:26:35.079
all around the world everyday.

01:26:35.079 --> 01:26:37.615
- Beat something up enough, it
doesn't get up again, right?

01:26:37.615 --> 01:26:39.784
You can exceed some threshold,

01:26:39.784 --> 01:26:41.919
and that's what we always
are concerned about

01:26:41.919 --> 01:26:43.988
with ecological resilience is:

01:26:43.988 --> 01:26:45.990
Where is that threshold of impact

01:26:45.990 --> 01:26:48.059
where the system does not bounce back?

01:26:48.059 --> 01:26:49.961
We've seen this before.

01:26:49.961 --> 01:26:54.899
One of the best examples is
Georges Bank, the cod fishery,

01:26:54.899 --> 01:26:56.200
one of the most tremendous fisheries

01:26:56.200 --> 01:26:57.468
on the face of the Earth,

01:26:57.468 --> 01:27:00.705
and we fished it darn
near to wiping it out.

01:27:00.705 --> 01:27:02.406
And there's still cod out there.

01:27:02.406 --> 01:27:04.975
We backed off the fishing pressure,

01:27:04.975 --> 01:27:07.111
but those populations
aren't growing, all right?

01:27:07.111 --> 01:27:10.448
We've exceeded some resilience threshold.

01:27:10.448 --> 01:27:11.982
They're not responding.

01:27:11.982 --> 01:27:14.185
There's no resilience there
in the cod populations.

01:27:14.185 --> 01:27:18.122
There's something that
we've done by going too far,

01:27:18.122 --> 01:27:19.423
too much of an impact.

01:27:19.423 --> 01:27:21.358
Have we done that to the Gulf of Mexico?

01:27:21.358 --> 01:27:24.228
To even one part of it or all of it?

01:27:25.129 --> 01:27:26.464
Have we exceeded thresholds

01:27:26.464 --> 01:27:28.866
for the Gulf of Mexico ecosystem?

01:27:28.866 --> 01:27:30.668
We won't know for year.

01:27:30.668 --> 01:27:33.003
- It means that this generation,

01:27:33.003 --> 01:27:36.307
that we pride ourselves in
being such progressives,

01:27:36.307 --> 01:27:39.410
that we've changed so much
stuff, the Baby Boomers.

01:27:39.410 --> 01:27:43.080
It means that everything we told them,

01:27:43.080 --> 01:27:44.081
they have to ignore.

01:27:45.149 --> 01:27:47.384
It means that all these promises we made

01:27:47.384 --> 01:27:49.386
about how right we were,

01:27:49.386 --> 01:27:51.922
we have to admit we were wrong.

01:27:51.922 --> 01:27:53.891
Did we change come great things? Sure!

01:27:55.259 --> 01:27:56.927
I'd love to say we brought

01:27:56.927 --> 01:27:59.163
about Civil Rights to some degree.

01:27:59.163 --> 01:28:00.831
We did, we helped.

01:28:00.831 --> 01:28:05.069
I'd love to say that we gave women rights

01:28:05.069 --> 01:28:07.938
that they didn't have before today.

01:28:07.938 --> 01:28:09.940
Yeah, I'd like to say that.

01:28:09.940 --> 01:28:12.910
We had a positive social impact,

01:28:12.910 --> 01:28:17.248
but if you weigh the bad
decisions we made along the way,

01:28:17.248 --> 01:28:20.384
if you weigh the
consumerism that ate us up,

01:28:21.719 --> 01:28:24.855
if you weigh the culture that we built

01:28:24.855 --> 01:28:28.525
that is around celebrity,
it's around celebrity.

01:28:30.194 --> 01:28:33.697
It's around ideas that
don't really advance us

01:28:33.697 --> 01:28:36.634
as a culture, they entertain us,

01:28:36.634 --> 01:28:38.936
and we're able to say...

01:28:40.304 --> 01:28:42.573
We're able to say we've
made some advances,

01:28:42.573 --> 01:28:45.209
I suppose, as a culture
entertaining ourselves,

01:28:45.209 --> 01:28:46.410
making ourselves comfortable,

01:28:46.410 --> 01:28:48.579
building our houses, making our car.

01:28:48.579 --> 01:28:50.914
We've done some good things, I suppose,

01:28:50.914 --> 01:28:53.717
but we have to tell, I
have to tell my child

01:28:53.717 --> 01:28:56.420
to ignore most of the things I've done

01:28:56.420 --> 01:29:00.357
because if she doesn't
ignore the things we've made

01:29:00.357 --> 01:29:02.893
as a Baby Boomer generation,

01:29:02.893 --> 01:29:05.362
it's endgame time.

01:29:05.362 --> 01:29:08.632
(pensive piano melody)

01:29:42.199 --> 01:29:45.869
(dramatic foreboding music)

01:32:53.223 --> 01:32:56.793
(typewriter keys clicking)

01:32:58.228 --> 01:32:59.362
- They could burn in hell,

01:32:59.362 --> 01:33:01.398
is the way I feel about 'em right now.

01:33:01.398 --> 01:33:02.232
That's the way I feel about it.

01:33:02.232 --> 01:33:04.100
They could burn in hell,
what they're doing to me now.

01:33:04.100 --> 01:33:06.603
You know, hey, I'm fed up with it.

01:33:07.670 --> 01:33:08.905
They could burn in hell.

